---------------------------------------------------- WEB DEVELOPMENT ---------------------------------------------------------

Introduction to Web Development
 Web Development is the process of creating and maintaining websits and web pages

Types of Web development:

1.Frontend development
  Creates the user interface (UI)
  Everything the user can see and interact with

 Technologies:
 HTML
 CSS
 JavaScript

2.Backend Development
 Handles server-side logic
 Manages databases, authentication, and APIs

 Technologies:
 Node.js
 Python
 Java
 PHP
 MySQL
 MongoDB

3.Full Stack Development
 Works on both Frontend and Backend

What is a Web Browser?
 A web browser is software used to access and display websites.
Examples:
 Google Chrome
 Microsoft Edge
 Mozilla Firefox
 Safari

What is a Web Server?
A web server stores website files and sends them to users when requested.
Examples:
 Apache
 Nginx

Website:
 A collection of interconnected web pages

Webpage:
 A single page of a website

URL (Uniform Resource Locator):
 The address of a webpage

HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol):
 The protocol used for communication between the browser and the web server

HTTPS:
 A secure version of HTTP

HTML (HyperText Markup Language)
 HTML is the standard markup language used to create the structure of web pages
 HTML is not a programming languages
 It tells the browser how to display content on a webpages

What can HTML create?
 Headings
 Paragraphs
 Images
 Links
 Tables
 Lists
 Forms
 Buttons
 Videos
 Audio

HTML File Extension:
 HTML files are saved with the .html extension

Examples:
 index.html
 about.html
 contact.html
 
Basic HTML structur:
 Every HTML document follows a basic structure
Syntax:

 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>
 <head>
     <title> My website </title>
 </head>

 <body>
     
      hii

 </body>
 </html>

<!DOCTYPE html>
 Declares that the document is an HTML document
 It helps the browser render the webpage correctly

<html>
 The root element of an HTML document
 All HTML code is written inside this tag

<head>
 Contains information about the webpage
 This information is not displayed on the webpages

Examples:
 Title
 Meta tags
 CSS files
 JavaScript files

<title>
 Define the title of the webpage
 The title appears on the browser Tab

Example:
 <title> My website </title>

<body>
 Contains all the visible content of the webpage

Examples:
 Headings
 Paragraphs
 Images
 Links
 Tables
 Forms
 Lists

HTML tags
 HTML uses tags to define elements

 Most tags have:
 Opening tag
 <tag>
 closing tag
 </tag>

Example:
 <p>Hello World</p>

Nested tags:
 HTML tags can be placed inside other tags
Example:
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>welcome</h1>
        </body>
    </html>

HTML tags:
 HTML provides 6 levels of heading to define titles and subtitles on a webpage

Syntax
 <h1>Heading 1</h1>   -> Largest and most important heading
 <h2>Heading 2</h2>   -> Second largest heading  
 <h3>Heading 3</h3>   -> Third largest heading
 <h4>Heading 4</h4>   -> Fourth largest heading
 <h5>Heading 5</h5>   -> Fifth largest  heading
 <h6>Heading 6</H6>   -> Smallest heading

Paragraph:
 The <p> tag is used to create a paragraph

Syntax:
 <p> paragraph </p>

Anchor Tag(Links):
 The <a> tag is used to create hyperlinks

Syntax:
 <a href="URL">Link Name</a>
 
href Attribute:
 The href attribute specifies the destination (URL) of the Link

Opening a Link in a New Tab:
 Use the target="_blank" attribute

Examples:
 <a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">Google</a>

HTML Attributes
 Attributes provide additional information about HTML elements

Syntax:
 <tag attribute="value">Content</tag>

Example:
 <a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>

 href -> Attribute
 "https://www.google.com" -> Value

Line Break:
 The <br> tag moves the content to the next Line

Horizontal Line:
 The <hr> tag inserts a horizontal Line

HTML Comments
 Comments are not displayed on the webpages

Syntax:
 <!-- This is a comment -->

Image in HTML:
 The <img> tag is used to display an image on a webpage

Syntax:
 <img src ="image.jpg" alt="Image Description">

Attributes:
->arc(Source)
 Specifies the path of the image
  
->alt (Alternative Text)
 Displays text if the image cannot be loaded
 Improves accessibility

Examples:
 <img src="hello.jpg" alt="hello">

Image Width and Height:
 The width and height attributes are used to set the image size

Example:
 <img src="cat.jpg" a;t="cat" width="300" height="200">

Lists in HTML:
 HTML provides three types of Lists

1.Ordered List (<ol>)
  Display items in numbered Order

Example:
   <ol>
       <li>web</li>
       <li>banana</li> 
       <li>development</li>
    </ol>

2.Unordered List(<ul>)
  Display items using bullets

Example:
    <ul>
       <li>web</li>
       <li>mango</li>
       <li>development</li>
     </ul>

3.Description List(<dl>)
 Used to display terms and their descriptions

Tags Used:
 <dl> -> Description List
 <dt> -> Description term
 <dd> -> Description Definition

Example:
 <dl>
    <dt>HTML</dt>
    <dd>HyperText Markup Language</dd>

    <dt>CSS</dt>
    <dd>Cascading Style Sheets</dd>
  </dl>

Tables in HTML:
 The <Table> tag is used to create Tables

Structure:
  <table>
       <tr>
           <th>Name</th>
           <th>Age</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Abhishek</td>
            <td>20</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

Table Tags:

<Table> -> Define a table

<tr> -> Defines a table row

<th> -> Defines a table heading

<td> -> Defines table data (cell)

Colspan Attribute
 The colspan attribute allows a cell to span multiple columns

Example:
   <td colspan="2">Student Details</td>

rowspan Attribute
 The rowspan attribute allows a cell to span multiple rowspan

Example:
    <td rowspan="2">Abhishek</td>

SEO:
 SEO stands for Search Engine Optimization

 SEO is the process of improving a website so that it can rank better in search engine results

Why is SEO important?
 Improve website visibility
 Helps search engines understand the website
 Increases organic traffic
 Helps users find relevant content

Important SEO Practices
1.Title:
 The <title> tag defines the title of a webpage

2.Meta Description 
 Meta description provides a short description of the webpage

Example:
 <meta name="description" content="This is my personal website">

3.Headings
 HTML provides heading from <h1> to <h6>

 <h1>Main Heading</h1>
 <h2>Sub Heading</h2>
 <h3>Another Heading</h3>

 Headings should be used in a proper hierarchy

4.Semantic HTML
 Semantic HTML uses meaningful tags that describe the purpose of the content

Examples:
 <header>
 <nav>
 <main>
 <section>
 <article>
 <footer>

Coree Web Vitals:
 Core web vitals are metrics used to measure  important asppects of a webpage's user experience

There are three main Core Web Vitals:
1.LCP -> Largest Contentful Paint
 Measure how quickly the largest visible content loads
 Focus -> Loading Performance

2.INP ->Interaction to Next Paint
 Measures how quickly a webpage responds to user Interaction
 Focus -> Responsiveness

3.CLS -> Cumulative Layout Shift
 Meaasures unexpected movement of elements on a webpage while Loading 
 Focus -> Visual Stability

HTML Forms
 AA form  is used to collect input and information from users

Examples:
 Login Form
 Refistration Form
 Search Form
 Feedback Form

 The <form> tag is used to create a form

Basic Syntax:
 <form action="backend.php">

 </form> -> Action specifies where the form data should be sent for processing

Input Tag:
 The <input>  tag is used to create interactive input fields in a form

Syntax:
 <input type="text">

The type attribute specifies the type of input

Common Input Type:
 text
 email
 password
 number
 date 
 radio
 checkbox
 submit

Examples:
 <input type="text">
 <input type="email">
 <input type="password">
 <input type="number">
 <input type="date">

Name Attribute:
 The name attribute is used to identify the input field when the form data is submitted

Example:
 <input type="text" name="username">

GET:
 Sends data through the URL
 Data can be visible in the URL
 Suitable for non-sensitive data

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="get">

POST:
 Sends data in the request body
 Data is not displayed in the URL
 Commonly used for submitting sensitive or large amounts of data

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="post">

Label Tag:
 The <label> tag defines a label for an input field

Example:
 <label for="name">Name:</label>
 <input type="text" id="name" name="name">

 The for attribute of <label> should match the id of the input

Textarea:
 The <textarea> tag is used to create a multi-line text input field

Example:
 <textarea name="message" rows="5" cols="30">
 </textarea>

it is usedful for:
 Comments
 Feedback
 Messages
 Addresses

Submit Buttom
 The submit input is used to submit the form

Example:
 <input type="submit" value="Submit">

Example:
 <form action="backend.php" method="post">
    
     <label for="name">Name:</label>
     <input type="text" id="name" name="name">

     <br><br>

     <label for="email">Email:</label>
     <input type="email" id="email" name="email">

     <br><br>

     label for="message">Message:</label>
     <textarea id="message" name="message"></textarea>

     <br><br>

     <input type="submit" value="Submit">

 </form>

INLINE AND BLOCK ELEMENTS IN HTML:
Block-Level Elements
 A block-level elements always starts on a new line and takes up the full available width
 Take full availabble width

Inline Elements:
 An inline element does not start on a new line
 It takes only as much width as necessary

Block Elements (Most Commonly Used First)
 <div>: A generic container for flow content.
 <p>: Paragraph.
 <h1>, <h2>, <h3>, <h4>, <h5>, <h6>: Headings.
 <ul>: Unordered list.
 <ol>: Ordered list.
 <li>: List item.
 <form>: A section containing form controls.
 <table>: Table.
 <section>: A standalone section of a document.
 <header>: A container for introductory content or a set of navigational links.
 <footer>: Footer of a section or page.
 <nav>: A section of a page that contains navigation links.
 <article>: A self-contained composition in a document.
 <aside>: A section of a page that contains information indirectly related to the main content.
 <main>: The main content of a document.
 <fieldset>: A set of form controls grouped under a common name.
 <blockquote>: A block of text that is a quotation from another source.
 <pre>: Preformatted text.
 <canvas>: A container used to draw graphics via JavaScript.
 <dl>: Description list.
 <dt>: Term in a description list.
 <dd>: Description in a description list.
 <figure>: Any content that is referenced from the main content.
 <figcaption>: A caption for a <figure> element.
 <address>: Contact information for the author or owner of the document.
 <hr>: A thematic break or a horizontal rule.
 <tfoot>: Footer of a table.

Inline Elements (Most Commonly Used First)
 <a>: Anchor or hyperlink.
 <img>: Image.
 <span>: Generic inline container.
 <input>: Input field.
 <label>: Label for a form element.
 <strong>: Strong emphasis.
 <em>: Emphasized text.
 <br>: Line break.
 <code>: Code snippet.
 <b>: Bold text.
 <i>: Italic text.
 <u>: Underlined text.
 <small>: Smaller text.
 <sub>: Subscript.
 <sup>: Superscript.
 <mark>: Marked or highlighted text.
 <q>: Short inline quotation.
 <cite>: Citation.
 <kbd>: Keyboard input.
 <samp>: Sample output.
 <var>: Variable in a mathematical expression or programming context.
 <time>: Time.
 <abbr>: Abbreviation.
 <data>: Machine-readable translation of content.
 <acronym>: Acronym (Not supported in HTML5). 

IDS and Classes in HTML:

ID: An ID is a unique identifier used to identify one specific HTML element

Syntax:
 <p id="intro">Hello World</p>
 Here,intro is the ID of the paragraph

-> An id should be unique within a webpage
-> The same id should not be used for multiple Elements
-> An ID is useful when we want to identify a specific elements

Class: A class is used to group multiple HTML elements that share the same characteristics or purpose

Syntax:
 <p class="red">Hello</p>
 <p class="red">Welcome</p>
 Both paragraphs belong to the same class

-> The same class can be used on multiple elements
-> An element can have multiple Classes

Example:
 <p class="red bold">Hello</p>
 Here the paragraph has two classes:
 red
 bold

Using ID and Class Together:
 An HTML element can have both an ID and a class

 <p id="first" class="text">
     Hello World
 </p>

Here:
 id -> first
 classs -> text

Multiple Classes:
 An element can have more than one class
 <p class="text red bold">
    Hello World
 </p>
 The classes are seeperated by spaces
 text
 red
 bold

Why are IDs and Classes Used:
 IDs and classes are mainly used to:
 Identify HTML elements
 Group HTML elements
 Apply CSS styles
 Select elements using JavaScript

Example:
 <h1 id="title" class="heading">
     My website
 </h1>

 id -> uniquely identifies the heading
 class -> identifies the group/category of the element

Video Audio and Media in HTML:
HTML  allows us to add different types of media to a webpage, such as:
 Images
 Audio
 Video
 Embedded Content

1.Video in HTML
 The <video> tag is used to add a video to a webpage

Syntax:
 <video src="video.mp4" controls></video>

controls
 The controls attribute displays video controls such as:
 Play/Pause
 Volume
 FullSCREEN
 progress bar 

2.Video Attributes 
 Some commonly used attributes of the <video> tag are:

controls
 Display video controls
 <video src="video.mp4" controls></video>

autoplay
 automatically starts the video
 <video src="video.mp4" autoplay></video>

loop
 Plays the video repeatedly
 <video src="video.mp4" loop></video>

muted
 Starts the video without sound
 <video src="video.mp4" muted></video>

poster
 Displays an image before the video starts
 <video src="video.mp4" poster="thumbnail.jpg controls></video>

3.Width and Height
 We can specify the size of a video using width and height

Syntax:
 <video src="video.mp4"
        width="500"
        height="300"
        controls></video>
        
4.Audio in HTML
 The <audio> tag is used to add audio to a webpage

syntax:
 <audio src="song.mp3" controls></audio>
 The controls attribute provides audio controls such as:
 play/Pause
 Volume
 progress bar

5.Audio Attributes
controls
 Display src="song.mp3" controls></audio>

autoplay
 Automatically starts the audio
 <audio src="song.mp3" autoplay></audio>

loop 
 Repeats the audio continuously
 <audio src="song.mp3" loop></audio>

muted
 Starts the audio without sound
 <audio src="song.mp3" muted></audio>

6.Souce Tag
 The <souce> tag is used to specify media files inside <video> or <audio>
 
Example:
  <video controls>
         <source src="video.mp4" type="video/mp4">
  </video>

Audio:
 <audio controls>
        <source src="song.mp3" type="audio/mpeg">
 </audio>
 using <source> can allow us to provide different media formats

7.Iframe
 The <iframe> tag is used to embed another webpage or external content inside our webpage

Example:
 <iframe src="https://example.com"></iframe>
 youtube Videos
 Maps
 Other webpages

Semantic Tags in HTML:
 Semantic tags are HTML tags that clearly describe the meaning and purpose of their content

They make the structure of a webpage easier to understand for:
 Developers
 Browsers
 Search engines
 Screen readers

Example:
 <header>
      <h1>My Website</h1>
 </header>
 Here, <header> clearly tells us that the content is the header section of the Webpage

Why use Semantic Tags?
 Makes HTML cade easier to read and unserstand
 Gives meaning to different sections of a webpage
 Improves accessibility
 Helps search engines understand the webpage structure
 Makes website structure more organized

Important Semantic Tags:
1.<header>
 Represents the introductory/header section of a webpage or section

Example:
 <header>
      <h1>My Website</h1>
 </header>

2.<nav>
 Represents navigation links

Example:
 <nav>
    <a href="index.html">Home</a>
    <a href="about.html">About</a>
    <a href="contact.html">Contact</a>
 </nav>


3.<main>
 Represents the main content of the webpage

Example:
 <main>
    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    <p>This is the main content.</p>
 </main>
 A page should generally have one main <main> element

4.<section>
 Represent a separate section of related content

Example:
 <section>
    <h2>About Me</h2>
    <p>I am a web developer.</p>
 </section>

5.<article>
 Represents independent, self-Contained content

Example:
 Blog post
 News article
 Forum post
 Product/article content

Example:
 <article>
    <h2>My First Blog</h2>
    <p>This is my first blog post</p>
 </article>

6.<aside>
 Represents content related to the main content to the main content but not part of the main flow

Examples:
 Sidebar
 Related links
 Additional information

Example:
 <aside>
    <h3>Related Links</h3>
    <a href='#'>HTML Tutorial</a>
 </aside>

7.<footer>
 Represents  the footer section of a webpage or section
 Usually contains:
 Copyright information
 Contact information
 Links
 Other additional information

Example:
 <footer>
   <p>Copyright 2026 My website</p>
 </footer>

Semantic Structure:
 A webpage can be organized like this

 +-------------------------+
 |         HEADER          |
 +-------------------------+
 |          NAV            |
 +-------------------------+
 |                         |
 |          MAIN           |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |   |    SECTION      |   |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |                         |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |   |    ARTICLE      |   |
 |   +-----------------+   |
 |                         |
 +-------------------------+
 |         ASIDE           |
 +-------------------------+
 |         FOOTER          |
 +-------------------------+

Non-Semantic Tags
 Do not clearly describe the meaning of their content

Example:
 <div>
 <span>

Entities and Code Tag in HTML
1.HTML Entities
 HTML entities are special codes used to display reserved characters and special symbols in HTML
  Some characters have a special meaning in HTML.

Example:
 <p>5 < 10</p>
 The < character can be interpreted as the beginning of an HTML tag.
 So we use an entity:
 <p>5 &lt; 10</p>
 Output:
 5 < 10

Common HTML Entities
 Entity Character	  Meaning
 &lt;	   <	      Less than
 &gt;	   >	      Greater than
 &amp;	   &	      Ampersand
 &quot;	   "	      Double quotation mark
 &apos;	   '	      Apostrophe
 &copy;	   ©	      Copyright
 &nbsp;	 Space	      Non-breaking space

2.Non-Breaking Space
 &nbsp; represents a non-breaking space
 Normally, multiple spaces written in HTML are collapsed into a single visible space

Example:
 <p>Hello   world</p>
 The browser does not preserve all those spaces
Using:
 <p>Hello%nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;world</p>
 creates visible spacing between the words

3.HTML Quotation Tags
 HTML provides tags for displaying quotations

<blockquote>
 Used for a longer quotation from another source
Example:
 <blockquote>
     Learning never stops
 </blockquote>

<q>
 Used for a short inline quotation
Example:
 <p> He said <q> Hello World</q>.</p>

4.<code> Tag
 The <code> tag is used to represent a piece of computer code
Example:
 <p> Use <code>cout</code> to print output in c++.</p>
 it is useful when displaying programming code on a webpage

5.<pre> Tag
 The <pre> tag displays preformatted text
 It preserves:
 Spaces
 Line breaks
 Formatting
Example:
 <pre>
 Hello
   World
     HTML
 </pre>
 The spacing and line breaks are preserved
 
6.<code> with <pre>
 For displaying multiple lines of code, <code> can be used inside <pre>
Example:
 <Pre>
 <code>
 #include &It;iostream&gt;
 using namespace std;

 int main()
 {
    cout &It;&It; "Hello";
 }
 </code>
 </pre>
 This is useful for displaying code on a webpage

7.Prism.js
 Prism.js is a javaScript library used for syntax highlighting of code
 Syntax highlighting makes different parts of code appear in different styles/colors, making code easier to read
 It is commonly used when displaying programming code on websites

8.Obsolete HTML Tags
 Some older HTML tags are obsolete/deprecated and should generally not be used in modern HTML
Example include:
 <center>
 <font>
 <big>
 Modern websites generally use CSS for styling instead
 For example, instead of using:
 <center>Hello</center>
 CSS should be used to control alignment

Introduction to CSS:
 CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets
 Css  is used to style and design HTML webpages
 Styling of webpage

CSS is used to:
 Change text color
 Change background color
 Change font size
 Change fonts
 Add borders
 Add spacing
 Change element size
 Position elements
 Create attractive webpages

CSS Syntax:
 Basic CSS syntax:
 selector {
       property: value;   
 }

Example:
 h1 {
   color: blue;
 }

 Here:
 h1 -> Selector
 color -> Property
 blue -> value

Three Ways to Add CSS:
1.Inline CSS
2.Internal CSS
3.External CSS

1.Inline CSS
 CSS is written inside the HTML element using the style attribute

Example:
 <h1 style="color: red;">Hello worls</h1>

Adavantage:
 Easy to apply styling to a single element

Disadvantage:
 Not suitable for styling many elements because it makes HTML code lengthy

2.Internal CSS
 CSS is written inside the <style> tag, usually inside the <head> section

Example:
 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>

 <head>

   <style>
       h1 {
            color: blue;
       }

       p {
         color: green;
       }

   </style>
 </head>
 <body>
     <h1>Hello world</h1>
     <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
 </body>
 </html>
 Internal CSS can style multiple elements on the same webpage

3.External CSS
 CSS is written in a seprate .css file

Example:
HTML file
 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>

 <head>
  
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

 </head>

 <body>
   <h1>Hello world</h1>
 </body>
 </html>

CSS file
 h1{
     color: blue;S
 }

Terms:
Selector -> Selects the HTML element that we want to style
Property -> Specifies what we want to change
Value -> Specifies the value of the property

CSS Selectors
 CSS Selectors are used to select HTML elements so that CSS styles can be applied to them

Example:
 p {
     color: red;
 }

Here:
 p -> Selector
 color -> Property
 red -> Value

Element Selector
 The Element Selector selects all HTML elements of a particular type

Example:
 div {
     background-color: red;
 }
 This selects all <div> elements

Example:
 <div>Hello</div>
 <div>Hello Again</div>

 Both <div> elements will get the same styling 

-> The drawback is that if there are multiple elements of the same type, the style is applied to all of them

Class Selector
 A Class Selector is Used to select elements having a particular class
 It is represented usinng: .

Example:
 HTML:
 <div class="red">
    Hello
    </div>

 .red {
    background-color: red;
 }

 Only elements with:
 class="red"
 will receive this style

Multiple elements can use the same class:
 <div class="red">One</div>
 <p class="red">Two</p>

Both can be styled using:
 .red{
   color: red;
 }

ID Selector
 An ID Selector selects an element using its id
 It is represented using:
 #
Example:
HTML:
 <div id="green">
      Hello
      </div>

CSS:
 #green {
     background-color: green;
 }

 The element with:
 id="green"
 will receive the style

 Class -> .
 ID    -> #

Example:
 .red { }
 #green { }

Child Selector 
 The Child Selector selects an element that is direct child of another element
 It uses:
 >

Example:
 div > p {
    color: blue;
 }

 This means:
   Select <p> elements that are direct children of <div>

Example:
 <div>
    <p>Hello</p>
  </div>

 Here <p> is a direct child of <div>, so the style applies

Important Difference
Consider:
 <div>
    <article>
       <p>Hello</p>
     </article>

  </div>

Here <p> is not a direct child of <div>

Therefore:
 div > p {
   color: blue;
 }
 will not select this <p>

Descendant Selector
 The Descendant Selector selects an element that exists anywhere inside another element
 It uses a space

Syntax:
 parent child {
    property: value;
 }

Example:
 div p {
    color: blue;
 }

 This selects <p> elements inside <div>, even if they are nested deeper 

Example:
 <div>
     <article>
         <p>Hello</p>
     </article>
 </div>

Here <p> is a descendant of <div>

Therefore:
 div p {
   color: blue;
 }
 will apply the style

Child vs Descendant Selector
 Child Selector
 div > p

Means:
 div
  ↓
  p
Only direct child

Descendant Selector
 div p
Means:
 div
  ↓ 
 article 
  ↓ 
  p

  Can select a child, grandchild, or deeper descendant

  >     -> Direct child
  space -> Any descendant

Universal Selector 
 The Universal Selector selects all HTML elements
 It is represented by:
 *
Example:
 * {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
 }
 This applies the style to all elements
 It is commonly used for basic CSS resets 

Pseudo-Class Selector
 A Pseudo-Class Selector selects an elementy based on a particular state or condition
 It uses:
 :
Example:
 a:hover {
    color: red;
 }
 When the mouse pointer moves over the link, the color changes to red

Common Pseudo-Classes
:hover
 Applies when the mouse is placed over an element
 a:hover {
    color: red;
 }

:visited
 Applies to a link that has already been visited
 a:visited {
   color: purple;
 }

:link 
 Selects an unvisited link
 a:link {
   color: blue;
 }

:active 
 Applies while the link is being activated/clicked
 a:active {
     color: green;
 }

:first-child 
 Selects an element if it is first child of its parent
Example:
 p:first-child {
     color: red;
 }

::first-line 
 ::first-line is a pseudo-element used to style the first line of text inside an element.

Example:
 p::first-line {
     color: yellow;
 }

 Only the first visible line of the paragraph gets the style.

 First line → Yellow
 Second line → Normal
 Third line → Normal

Attribute Selector 
 An attribute selector selects elements based on their attributes
 Select elements having an attribute
 [data-color] {
     border: 2px solid red;
 }
 This selects elements that contain the data-color attribute
Example:
 <div data-color="primary">Box</div>

Select a Specific Attribute Value 
 [data-color="primary] {
 margin-top: 45px;
}
This selects elements where:
 data-color = "primary"

Example:
 <div data-color="primary">Box 1</div>
 <div data-color="secondary">Box 2</div>
Only the first element is selected
Important:
Attribute selectors can be used with different attributes, not only data-* attributes

Grouping Selectors 
 Multiple selectors can be grouped together using a comma ,

Example:
 p, a, .box, [data-color="primary"] {
     margin-top: 20px;
 }

The same CSS rule is applied to all matching elements

Without grouping:
 p {
     margin-top: 20px;
 }

 a {
     margin-top: 20px;
 }

 .box {
     margin-top: 20px;
 }
 Grouping makes the CSS shorter 

 selector1, selector2, selector3 
              ↓ 
      Same CSS rule

:nth-child()
 :nth-child() selects an element based on its position among its siblings.

Example:
 .box:nth-child(2) {
     background-color: red;
 }
This selects the second child

Even Children 
 .box:nth-child(even) {
    background-color: blue;
 }

This selects:
 2nd
 4th
 6th
 8th
 ...

Odd Children 
 .box:nth-child(odd) {
    background-color: green;
}
This selects:
 1st
 3rd
 5th
 7th
 ...

:nth-last-child()
 :nth-last-child() works like :nth-child(), but counts from the end.

Example:
 .box:nth-last-child(1) {
     background-color: red;
 }

This selects the last child

Example:
 .box:nth-last-child(2) {
     background-color: blue;
 }
This selects the second-last child

Difference:
 :nth-child()
       ↓
 Counts from beginning

 :nth-last-child()
       ↓
 Counts from end

::before 
 ::before is a pseudo-element that allows us to insert generated content before an element's content.

Example:
 .box::before {
     content: "Hello ";
 }

The generated content appears before the existing content.

Important:
 When using ::before, the content property is normally required.

Example:
 .box::before {
     content: "→ ";
     color: red;
 }

::after 
 ::after inserts generated content after an element's content.

Example:
 .box::after {
     content: " ✓";
     color: green;
 }

If:
 <div class="box">Completed</div>

 It can visually appear like:
 Completed ✓

::first-letter 
 ::first-letter selects the first letter of an element's text.

Example:
 p::first-letter {
     font-size: 45px;
    color: purple;
 }

Only the first letter is styled
Example:
 H ello world
 ↑
 Styled

::placeholder 
 ::placeholder is used to style the placeholder text of an input.

Example:
 input::placeholder {
     color: blue;
 }

HTML:
 <input type="text" placeholder="Enter your name">
 The placeholder text becomes blue

Pseudo-Class vs Pseudo-Element 
Pseudo-Class
 Used to select an element based on its state or position

Examples:
 :first-child
 :nth-child()
 :nth-last-child()

Pseudo-Element
 Used to style a specific part of an element or generate content
Examples:
 ::first-line
 ::first-letter
 ::before
 ::after
 ::placeholder

Easy Difference:
 Pseudo-Class
       ↓
 Selects based on state/position

 Pseudo-Element
       ↓
 Styles a part / generated content
 
CSS Box Model
 The CSS Box Model describes how every HTML element is represented as a rectangular Box
 It consists of four main parts:

        ┌─────────────────────────────┐
        │           Margin            │
        │  ┌───────────────────────┐  │
        │  │        Border         │  │
        │  │  ┌─────────────────┐  │  │
        │  │  │     Padding     │  │  │
        │  │  │  ┌───────────┐  │  │  │
        │  │  │  │  Content  │  │  │  │
        │  │  │  └───────────┘  │  │  │
        │  │  └─────────────────┘  │  │
        │  └───────────────────────┘  │
        └─────────────────────────────┘

The four parts are:
 Content
 Padding
 Border
 Margin

Content
 Content is the actual data inside an element
 It can be:
 Text
 Image
 Video
 Other HTML content
 The width and height  properties normally control the content area's dimensions
Example:
 .box {
    width: 300px;
    height: 100px;
 }

Padding
 Padding is the space between the content and the border
Example:
 .box {
   padding: 20 px;
 }

Meaning:
  Border
        ↓ 
 ┌─────────────────┐ 
 │ Padding         │ 
 │ ┌───────────┐   │ 
 │ │ Content   │   │ 
 │ └───────────┘   │ 
 └─────────────────┘

 Padding creates internal space inside the element 

Margin  
 Margin is the space outside the border
 It creates space between an element and other elements
Example:
  .box {
    margin: 20px;
  }

         Margin
            ↓ 
      ┌─────────────┐ 
      │    Border   │ 
      │   Content   │ 
      └─────────────┘ 
            ↑ 
        Outside 

 padding -> Space inside the border
 Margin -> Space outside the border

Border
 A border surrounds the content and padding

Example:
  .box {
     border: 2px solid red;
  }

 A border can have:
 Border width
 Border Style
 Border color

Separate properties
 .box {
     border-width: 2px;
     border-style: solid;
     border-color: red;
 }

Shorthand
 Instead of writing all three:
 border: 2px Solid red;

Border Radius
 border-radius is used to make the corners of a box rounded

Example:
 .box {
    border: 2px solid black;
    border-radius: 10px;
 }
 Large valuess create more rounded corners

Margin values
 Margin can be applied to all four sides
 .box {
   margin: 20px;
 }

 This applies:
 Top    -> 20px
 Right  -> 20px 
 Bottom -> 20px
 Left   -> 20px

Margin With Four values
 .box {
    margin: 10px 20px 30px 40px;
 }
 The order is:
 Top -> Right -> Bottom -> Left

Margin With Two values
 .box {
    margin: 10px 20px;
 }
 
Meaning:
 Top + Bottom -> 10px
 Left + Right -> 20px

Individual Margin properties
 You can set each side separately:
 .box {
    margin-top: 10px;
    margin-right: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    margin-left: 40px;
 }

Padding Shorthand
 Padding works similarly to margin 

One value 
 padding: 20px;

All four sides:
 Top    -> 20px
 Right  -> 20px
 Bottom -> 20px
 Left   -> 20px

Two values
 Padding: 10px 20px;

 Top + Bottom -> 10px
 Left + right -> 20px

Four values
 Padding: 10px 20px 30px 40px;

Order:
 Top -> Right -> Bottom -> Left

Total width and Height
 The actual space occupied by an element can be larger than  its declared width and height

Total Width
 Total width =
 Content Width 
 + Left Padding 
 + Right Padding 
 + Left Border 
 + Right Border 
 + Left Margin 
 + Right Margin

Total Height
 Total Height =
 Content Height
 + Top Padding 
 + Bottom Padding 
 + Top Border 
 + Bottom Border 
 + Top Margin 
 + Bottom Margin

Example of Box Model Calculation
 Suppose:
    .box {
      width: 200px;
      padding: 15px;
      border: 10px solid red;
      margin: 5px;
    }

Total Width:
 200
 + 15 + 15
 + 10 + 10
 + 5 + 5
 = 260px

 So the total horizontal space is:
 260px

Margin collapse
 Margin Collapse occurs when vertical margins of certain-level elements overlap
 When two vertical margins collapse, the resulting margin is generally the larger of the two margins, rather than their sum 

Example:
 .box1 {
     margin-bottom: 30px;
 }

 .box2 {
     margin-top: 20px;
 }

You might expect:
 30px + 20px = 50px

 But when these vertical margins collapse:
 Maximum(30px, 20px)
 = 30px

 So the effective gap is generally:
 30px

Important Point About Margin Collapse
 Margin collapse mainly applies to vertical margins of block-level elements
 It does not mean that every pair of margins always collapses
 For example, horizontal margins do not collapse in the same Way

Important Difference
 Property	          Meaning
 content	          Actual content
 padding	          Space between content and border
 border	            Surrounds content and padding
 margin	            Space outside the border

Complete Example:
 <!DOCTYPE html>
 <html>
 <head>

 <style>

 .box
 {
   width: 20px;
   height: 100px;

   padding: 20px;

   border: 5px solid black;

   margin: 30px;

   border-radius: 10px;
 }
 </style>
 </head>
 <body>
 <div class="box">
     Hello World
 </div>

 </body>
 </html>

 Here:

 width         -> Content width
 Height        -> content height
 padding       -> space inside
 border        -> Boundary
 margin        -> Space outside
 border-radius -> Rounded corners

Font Family
 The font-family property is used to change the typeface of text

Example:
 h1 {
   font-family: Arial;
 }

You can provide multiple fonta as fallbacks:
 h1 {
   font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
 }
 If Arial is unavailable, the browser uses sans-serif

Common geneeric families:
 serif
 sans-serif
 monospace
 cursive
 fantasy

Font size
 The font-size property changes the size of text
Example:
 p {
  font-size: 20px;
 }

Commonly used units include:
 px
 em
 rem
 %
 For now, px is the simplest unit to understand 

Font Style
 The font-style property controls whether text is normal, italic, etc 

Example:
 p {
  font-style: italic;
 }

Common values:
 normal
 italic
 oblique

Font Weight
 The font-weight property controls the thickness of text
Example:
 p {
  font-weight: bold;
 }

Common values:
 normal
 bold
 lighter
 bolder

 Numeric values can also be used:
 p {
  font-weight: 700;
 }

Generally:
 400 -> normal
 700 -> bold 
 
Google Fonts
 Google Fonts can be used to add additional fonts to a website
 You can select a font from google Fonts and include it in your HTML/CSS
 One Common method is using a <link> in the <head>:

 <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

 Then use it in CSS:
 body {
   font-family: "Poppins", sans-serif;
 }    

Another method is using @import inside CSS:
 @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins&display=swap');)
Then:
 body {
   font-family: "Poppins", sans-serif;
 }

Line Height
 The Line-height property controls the vertical space between lines of text
Example:
 p {
     line-height: 1.5;
 }

It can also be given using a unit:
 p {
   line-height: 30px;
 }
 Increasing line-height can make patragraphs easier to read 

Letter spacing
 The letter-spacing property controls the space between individual characters
Example:
  h1 {
      letter-spacing: 2px;
  }

Negative values can also be used:
 h1 {
   letter-spacing: -1px;
 }

Text alignment
 The text-align property controls the horizontal alignment to text
Example:
 h1 {
   text-align: center;
 }

Common values:
 left
 right
 center
 justify

Example:
 p {
   tect-align:  justify;
 }

Text Transformation
 The text-transform property changes the capitalization of text
Uppercase
 p {
   text-transform: uppercase;
 }

Example:
 hello world

Becomes:
 HELLO World

Lowercase
 p {
   text-transform: lowercase;
 }

Capitalize
 p {
   text-transform: capitalize;
 } 
 The capitalizes the first letter of each word

Text Decoration
 The text-decoration property adds decorations to text
Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: underline;
 }

Common values:
 underline
 overline
 line-through
 none

Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: line-through;
 }

Text Decoration color
 The color of a text decoration can be changed separately

Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: underline;
   text-decoration-style: dotted;
 }

Common styles include:
 solid
 double
 dotted
 dashed
 wavy

Text Decoration thickness
 The thickness of the text decoration can be controlled using:
 text-decoration-thickness
Example:
 p {
   text-decoration: underline;
   text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
 }

Colors in CSS
 The color property changes the color of text

Example:
 p {
   color: red;
 }
 Background color can be changed using:
 background-color: yellow;

Color Keywords
 CSS provides predefined color name
Example:
 p {
   color: red;
 }

Other examples:
 blue
 green
 orange 
 purple 
 black 
 white 
 yellow
There are many predifined CSS color notation

Hexadecimal Colors
 Colors can be represented using hexadecimal notation
Example:
 p {
   color: #ff0000;
 }

Here:
 #ff0000 -> red

A common six-digit hex color follows:
 #RRGGBB
where:
 RR -> red
 GG -> green
 BB -> Blue 
 Each pair represents the intesity of that color component

Example:
 #000000 -> black
 #ffffff -> white
 #ff0000 -> red
 #00ff00 -> green
 #0000ff -> Blue

RGB Colors
RGB stands for:
 red
 green
 blue

Syntax:
 color: rgb(255, 0, 0);

 This represents red

Each component generally ranges from:
 0 -> 255

Example:
 color: rgb(0, 255, 0);
 Green

RGBA Colors
 RGBA adds an alpha channel to RGB
 R → Red
 G → Green
 B → Blue
 A → Alpha
Example:
 color: rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.5);
 Here the last value controls transparency

HSL Colors
 HSL stands for:
 H -> Hue
 S -> Saturation
 L -> Lightness

Syntax:
 color: hsl(0, 100%, 50%);
 HSL provides another way to describe colors

HSLA 
 HSLA adds an alpha value to HSL
 color: hsla(0, 100%, 50%, 0.5)
Here:
 H -> Hue
 S -> Saturation
 L -> Lightness
 A -> Alpha

Color Values Can Be Used in Other properties
 The same color formats can be used for properties such as:
 color
 background-color
 border-color
 text-decoration-color

Example:
 .box {
   color: white;
   background-color: #000000;
   border-color: red;
 }

CSS Specificity
 Specificity determines which CSS rule is appliedd when multiple CSS rules target the same element
 When multiple rules conflict, the rule with higher specificity generally gets higher priority

Example:
 p {
   color: red;
 } 

 p {
   color: blue;
 }
 Both rules have the same specificity
 Therefore, the rule written later  wins:
 color -> blue 

Specificity Hierarchy
 The basic specificity order is:
 Inline Style 
     ↓ 
 ID Selector 
     ↓ 
 Class / Attribute / Pseudo-class 
     ↓ 
 Element Selector 
     ↓ 
 Universal Selector

 Inline > ID > Class > Element > Universal

Specificity Values
 A simple way to undertand specificity is by assigning weights:

 Universal Selector -> 0
 Element Selector   -> 1
 Class Selector     -> 10
 Attribute Selector -> 10
 Pseudo-class       -> 10
 ID Selector        -> 100
 Inline Style       -> 1000

Example:
 p { }
 Specificity: 1

 .box { }
 specificity: 10

 #heading { }
 Specificity: 100

 Inline:
 <p style="color: red;">
 Specificity: 1000

Specificity Calculation
 Specificity can be calculated by adding the values of the selectors

Example:
 <h1 id="title" class="heading">
     Hello
 </h1>

CSS:
 h1.heading#title {
   color: red;
 }

Calculate:
 h1       -> 1
 .heading -> 10
 #title   -> 100

Total:
 1 + 10 + 100 = 111

Therefore:
 Specificity = 111

Specificity with Multiple Classes
Example:
 .box.red {
   color: red;
 }

Specificity:
 .box -> 10
 .red -> 10

Total = 20
 More selectors can increase specificity 

Specificity Tie 
 If two CSS rules have the same specificity, the rule that appears later in the CSS generally wins

Example:
 p (
   color: red;
 )

 p {
   color: blue;
 }

Both have:
 Specificity = 1
 The second rule comes later

Therefore:
 Final color -> blue

CSS Cascade
 CSS stands for:
 Cascading Style Sheets
 The cascade  is the process the browser uses to  decide which CSS declaration should win when multiple declarations apply to the same element
 The cascade considers several factors, including:
 Position and order of appearance
 Specificity
 Origin
 Importance

Position / Order of appearance
 If completing rules have the same specificity, the rule apparing later generally takes precedence
Example:
 h1 {
   color: red;
 }

 h1 {
   color: green;
 }

Final result:
 green

!important
 !important gives a Css declaration very high priority
Example:
 p {
   color: red !important;
 }
 If another rule tries to change the same property:
 p {
   color: blue;
 }
 the red rule with !important wins

Why Avoid Excessive !important?
 Althrough !important can override  competing rules, using it too much makes CSS difficult to maintain

Example:
 p {
   color: red !important;
 }

 p {
   color: blue !important;
 }
 Now debugging becomes more difficult because multiple rules have been given unusually high priority

Inline CSS and Specificity
 Inline CSS has very high specificity compared with normal selectors
Example:
 <p style="color: red;">
    Hello
   </p>

Normal CSS:
 P {
   color: blue;
 }
 The inline style normally wins:
 Final color ->> red

Example of Specificity conflict
Consider:
 
  <h1 id="title" class="heading">
      Hello
   </h1>

CSS:
  * {
   color: gray;
  }

  h1 {
   color: pink;
  }

  .heading {
   color: blue;
  }

  #title {
   color: red;
  }

Specificity:
 *        -> 0
 h1       -> 1
 .heading -> 10
 #title   -> 100

Specificity Calculation Example
Consider:
 a.harry.rohan[href]:hover {
   color: red;
 }

Calculate:
 a      -> 1
 .harry -> 10
 .rohan -> 10
 [href] -> 10
 :hover -> 10

Specificity vs Cascade
Specificity
 Determines the strebgth/weight of a selector

Cascade
 Determines which declaration wins when multiple declarations apply, considering factprs such as:
 Order
 Specificity
 Origin
 Importance

CSS units
 CSS units are used to define the size, spacing, and dimensions of HTML elements
Example:
 .box {
   width: 200px;
   height: 100px;
 }

  CSS Units 
       ↓ 
 ┌───────────────┐ 
 │               │
 Absolute      Relative 
 Units         Units

Absolute Units
 Absolute units represent fixed sizes
Common absolute units:
 cm 
 mm 
 in 
 pt 
 pc 
 px
 For web development, px (pixel) is the most commonly used absolute unit 

Pixel-px
 px stands a fixed CSS length

Example:
 .box {
   width: 200px;
   height: 100px;
 }

Here:
 width -> 200 pixels
 height -> 100 pixels

Use of px
 Useful when we need relatively precise sizes, such as:
 border: 2px solid black;

Relative Units
 Relative units change their size based on another value, such as:
 Parent element
 Root element
 Viewport
 They are useful for creating responsive layouts

Important relative units:
 %
 em
 rem
 Vw 
 vh 
 vmin 
 vmax 

Percentage- %
 % is generally relative to the parent element's corresponding size
Example:
 .container {
   width: 500px;
 }

 .box {
   width: 50%;
 }

 The .box will be 50% of its containing block's width 

So approximately:
 Parent width = 500px
 50% = 250px 

em
 em is a relative unit based on the font size of the element's parent for font-size calculations
Example:
 .parent {
   font-size: 20px;
 }

 .child {
   font-size: 2em;
 }

Then:
 2em = 2 × 20px
     = 40px 

rem
 rem stands for Root EM 
 It is relative to the font size of the root element (html)
Example:
 html {
   font-size: 16px;
 }   

 .box {
   font-size: 2rem;
 }

Then:
 2rem = 2 × 16px 
      = 32px

vw
 vw stands for viewport width
 1vw = 1% of viewport width

Example:
 .box {
   width: 50vw;
 }

 This makes the width equal to 50% of the viewport width 
 If viewport width is:
 1000px
 then:
 50vw = 500px 

vh
 vh stands for viewport height 
 1vh = 1% of viewport height
Example:
 .box {
   height: 50vh;
 }
 If viewport height is:
 800px
 then:
 50vh = 400px

vmin
 vmin represents 1% of the smaller dimension of the viewport 
 It uses whichever is smaller:
 viewport width
        OR 
 viewport height
Example:
 .box {
   width: 50vmin;
 }

If:
 viewport width = 1000px
 viewport height = 800px
 Then the smaller dimension is:
 800px
Therefore:
 1vmin = 8px 
 50vim = 400px 

vmax
 vmax represents 1% of the larger dimension of the viewport
 It uses whichever is larger:
 viewport width
      OR 
 viewport height

Example:
 .box {
   width: 50vmax;
 }

If:
 viewport width = 100px
 viewport height = 800px 

 The larger dimension is:
 1000px

Therefore:
 1vmax = 10px
 50vmax = 500px 

ex and ch 
 These are less commonly used relative units
ex
 ex is related to the x-height of the current font
ch
 ch is based on the width of the 0 character in the current font 

Example:
 .box {
   width: 40ch;
 }
 This can be useful when controlling text-related widths

Other CSS Units
 Some other CSS units include:

 cm → Centimeters
 mm → Millimeters
 in → Inches
 pt → Points
 pc → Picas
 ex → x-height
 ch → width of "0"

Absolute vs Relative
 Absolute	Relative
 px	          %
 cm	          em
 mm	          rem
 in	          vw
 pt	          vh
 pc	          vmin

Display Property
 The CSS display property controls how an HTML element is displayed and how it participates in the layout
Syntax:
 selector {
  display: value;
 }

Example:
 .box {
   display: block;
 } 

Main Display Values
 The most important values are:
 display 
   ↓ 
 ├── block 
 ├── inline 
 ├── inline-block 
 └── none

Display: block 
 A block-level element:
 Starts on a new line
 Usually takes the full available width
 width and height can be applied 
Example:
 <div class="box">Hello</div>
 <div class="box">world</div>

 .box {
  display: block;
 }

Output structure:
 Hello
 World
Each element starts on a new line
Common clock-level elements include:
 <div>
 <p>
 <h1> to <h6>
 <section>
 <header>
 <footer>

Display: inline
 An inline element:
 Does not start on a new line
 Takes only the space required by its content
 width and height generally do not apply as they do for block-level elements

Example:
 <span>Hello</span>
 <span>world</span>

 span {
     display: inline;
 }

Output Structure:
 Hello World

Common inline elements include:
 <span>
 <a>
 <strong>
 <em>

Display: Inline-block
 inline-block combines features of both inline and block
 It:
 Stays in the same line like an inline element
 Allows width and height to be set like a block-level element
 Allows padding and margins to be applied

Example:
.box {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 200px;
  height: 100px;
}
 
 Multiple boxes can appears in the same line:
 ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ 
 │ Box 1    │ │ Box 2    │ 
 └──────────┘ └──────────┘

 inline       -> Same line
 block        -> New line
 inline-block -> Same line + width/height

display: none
 display: none removes the element from the layout
Example:
 .box {
  display: none;
 }

The element:
 Is not displayed
 Does not occupy space in the layout

Example:
 <p>Hello</p>
 <p class="hide">Hidden text</p>
 <p>world</p>
 .hide {
  display: none;
 }

 The result behaves like:
 Hello
 World
 The hidden element does not leave an empty space

display: none vs visibility: hidden
 These two are different
display: none
 display: none;
 Element is hidden and its space is removed

visibility: hidden
 visibility: hidden;
 Element is hidden, but its space remains reserved

Example:
 display: none:
 Hello
 World

 visibility: hidden:

 Hello
 [empty space]
 world

Changing Display Type
 CSS can change the default display behavior of an HTML element
Example:
 <span class="box">Hello</span>
 Normally <span> is inline

We can make it vlock:
 .box {
   display: block;
 }

Similarly, a block element can be made inline:
 .box {
  display: inline;
 }

Block vs Inline vs Inline-Block
 Property	               Block	    Inline	                             Inline-block
 New line	               Yes	      No	                                 No
 Takes available width	 Usually	  No	                                 No
 Width/Height	           Can set	  Generally not applied as for block   Can set
 Same line	             No	        Yes	                                 Yes

CSS Shadow
 Css shadows are used to add depth and visual effects to elements and text
 There are two important shadow properties:
 box-shadow
 text-shadow

box-shadow
 box-shadow adds a shadow around an element's box
Basic Syntax:
 box-shadow: offset-x offset-y color;
Example:
 .card {
   box-shadow: 10px 13px green;
 }
Here:
 10px -> Horizontal offset
 13px -> Vertical offset
 green -> Shadow color

box-shadow with Blur
 A blur radius can  be addded to make the shadow software
Syntax:
 box-shadow: offset-x offset-y blur-redius color;
Example:
 .card {
   box-shadow: 7px 5px 10px green;
 }
Here:
 7px -> Horixontal offset
 5px -> Vertical offset
 10px -> Blur radius
 green -> color

Spread Radius
 The spread radius controls how much the shadow expands or shrinks
Syntax:
 box-shadow: offset-x offset-y blur-radius spread-radius color;

Example:
 .card {
  box-shadow: 5px 5px 10px 20px green;
 }
Here:
 5px -> X offset
 5px -> Y offset
 10px -> Blur
 20px -> Spread
 green -> Color

inset 
 The inset keyword makes the shadow appears inside the element instead of outside

Example:
 .card {
  box-shadow: inset 3px 5px green;
 }

Multiple Box shadows
 Multiple shadows can be applied by separating them with commas

Example:
 .card {
  box-shadow:
   3px 5px green,
   4px 6px reed;
 }

 Each shadow is represented by: ,

Negative Shadow Values
 Negative offset values move the shadow in the opposite direction

Example:
 .card {
  box-shadow: -10px -13px green;
 }
 The shadow moves towars the left and upward

text-shadow
 text-shadow is used to add a shadow to text 

Syntax:
 text-shadow: offset-x offset-y color;
Example:
 h2 {
  text-shadow: 3px 4px red;
 }

Text Shadow with Blur
 A blur radius can also be used with text-shadow 
Example:
 h2 {
  text-shadow: 3px 2px 2px white;
 }
Syntax:
 offset-X
 offset-Y
 blur-radius
 color

Multiple Text Shadow
 Multiple shadows can also be applied to text by separating them with commas 
Example:
 h1 {
  text-shadow:
   2px 2px 3px red,
   -2px -2px 3px blue;
 }
 This can be used to create different visual effects 

CSS Outline
 An outline ia a line drawn around an element, outside its border 
Syntax:
 outline: width style color;

Example:
 .box {
  outline: 2px solid red;
 }

Outline vs Border
 Both border and outline draw a line around an element, but they are different

Border 
 border: 2px solid red;
 A border  is part of the element's box and can affect its dimensions/layout 

outline
 Outline: 2px solid red;
 An outline is drawn outside the element and does not take up space in the normal layout 

Outline styles
 Outline supports styles such as:
 solid
 dashed
 dotted
 double

Example:
 .box {
  outline: 3px dashed black;
 }

Outline-offset 
 outline-offset controls the distance between the element and its outline 
Example:
 .box {
   outline: 3px dashed black;
   outline-offset: 5px;
 }

Here:
 3px -> Outline width 
 dashed -> outline style
 black -> outline color 
 5px -> Distance from element 

Outline and Focus
 Outlines are commonly useful for showing which element currently has keyboard focus 
Example:
 buttton:focus {
   outline: 2px solid blue;
 }
 This makes the focused button visually identifiable 

Styling Lists 
 CSS Provides properties to customize the appearence of HTML lists 
 The main property is:
 list-style-type
 It controls the marker/bullet style of list items

list_style_type
Unordered List
 For <ul>, common values are:
 disc → ● 
 circle → ○ 
 square → ■ 
 none → No marker

Example:
 ul {
  list-style-type: square;
 }

HTML:
 <ul>
    <li>Home</li>
    <li>About</li>
    <li>Contact</li>
  </ul>

Ordered List
 For <ol>, list-style-type can change the numbering style
Common values:
 decimal              → 1, 2, 3
 decimal-leading-zero → 01, 02, 03
 lower-roman          → i, ii, iii
 upper-roman          → I, II, III
 lower-alpha          → a, b, c
 upper-alpha          → A, B, C

Example:
 ol {
  list-style-type: upper-roman;
 }

Output:
 I.   Home 
 II.  About 
 III. Contact

list-style-position
 The list-style-position property controls where the list marker is positioned

Two main values:
inside
 The marker is placed inside the content area
 ul {
  list-style-position: inside;
 }

outside
 The marker is placed outside the content area 
 ul {
  list-style-position: outside;
 }
 Outside is the default behavior for normal lists 

Removing List Markers
 To remove bullets or numbers:
  ul {
    list-style-type: none;
  }

Or using the shorthand:
 ul {
  list-style: none;
 }
 This is commonly used when creating navigation bars 

Example:
 ul {
  list-style: none;
 }
 
Using an Image as a list Markers
 Css can use an image as the marker of a list item
Property:
 list-style-image

Example:
 ul {
     list-style-image: url("arrow.png");
 }
 Now the image is used instead of the normal bullet.
 
Important
 The image should be placed inside the url() function:
 url("image.png")

list-style Shorthand
 Instead of writing multiple list-style properties separately, CSS provides the shorthand:
 list-style

Example:
 ul {
    list-style: square inside;
 }
It can combine list-style properties in one declaration

Custom List Markers
 A custom value can also be used as a list marker.
Example:
ul {
    list-style-type: "🔥";
 }
Output idea:
 🔥 Home
 🔥 About
 🔥 Contact
 This allows custom symbols/emojis to be used as markers

Creating a Navbar Using a List 
 Lists are commonly used to create navigation bars
HTML:
 <nav>
    <ul>
        <li>Home</li>
        <li>About</li>
        <li>Contact</li>
    </ul>
 </nav>

First remove the default bullets:
 ul {
     list-style: none;
 }

Then make the list items appear horizontally:
 li {
     display: inline;
 }

Now the items can appear like:
 Home    About    Contact

Basic Navbar Example
 ul { 
    list-style: none;
 }
 li { 
    display: inline;
    margin: 10px;
 }

Why Lists are useful for navbars 
 Lists provide a structured way to group navigation links

Example:

 <nav>
    <ul>
        <li>Home</li>
        <li>About</li>
        <li>Services</li>
        <li>Contact</li>
    </ul>
 </nav>

 CSS can then remove the default list markers and arrange the items horizontally
 
Overflow 
 Overflow occurs when the content inside an element is larger than the available size of that element
Example:
 .box {
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
 }
 If the content is larger than this area, it can overflow outside the box
 The CSS overflow property controls what happens to this extra content
 
Syntax:
 selector {
    overflow: value;
 }
Example:
 .box {
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
    overflow: hidden;
 }
 
overflow: visible 
 visible is the default value
 The overflowing content remains visible outside the element
Example:
 .box {
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
    overflow: visible;
 }
Concept:
 ┌──────────────┐
 │ Content      │
 │ Content      │
 └──────────────┘
       ↓
 Extra content can remain visible
 outside the box

overflow: scroll 
 scroll adds scrollbars so that the overflowing content can be accessed
Example:
 .box {
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
    overflow: scroll;
 }

Concept:
 ┌──────────────┐
 │ Content      │
 │ Content      │
 │ Content      │
 │          ↕   │
 └──────────────┘
 The user can scroll to see the hidden content

overflow: auto 
 auto adds scrollbars when necessary 
Example:
 .box {
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
    overflow: auto;
 }
 If the content fits:
 No scrolling needed
 If the content overflows:
 Scrollbar appears

visible → Show overflow 
hidden → Hide overflow 
scroll → Add scrolling 
auto → Scroll only when needed

overflow-X
 overflow-x controls overflow in the horizontal direction.

Example:
 .box {
     overflow-x: scroll;
 }

It controls the:
 ← Horizontal →
 overflow

overflow-y 
 overflow-y controls overflow in the vertical direction.

Example:
 .box {
     overflow-y: scroll;
 }

It controls:
 ↑
 │
 │ Vertical
 │
 ↓
 overflow

Overflow-x and overflow-y Together 
 You can control both directions separately
Example:
 .box {
     overflow-x: hidden;
     overflow-y: auto;
 }
 
This means:
 Horizontal → Hidden
 Vertical   → Auto

Overflow Shorthand 
 The overflow property can control both horizontal and vertical overflow.
Example:
 .box {
    overflow: hidden;
}

This applies to both:
 overflow-x
 overflow-y

You can also provide two values:
 .box {
    overflow: hidden auto;
 }

Here:
 First value  → overflow-x
 Second value → overflow-y

So:
 overflow-x → hidden
 overflow-y → auto

Position Property
 The position property is used to control how an element is positioned on a webpage.
Syntax:
 selector {
    position: value;
 }

Main values:
 static
 relative
 absolute
 fixed
 sticky

Position can be controlled using:
 top
 right
 bottom
 left

position: static 
 static is the default position of an element
 The element follows the normal flow of the webpage
Example:
 .box {
     position: static;
 }
With static positioning, properties like:
 top
 right
 bottom
 left
 do not move the element as they do with positioned elements

Position: relative
 A relatively positioned element is moved relative to its original position.
Example:
 .box {
     position: relative;
     left: 30px;
     top: 20px;
 }

The element moves:
 30px → right
 20px → down

Important Point
 The original space occupied by the element is still preserved.

Example:
 Before:
 [ Box 1 ]
 [ Box 2 ]
 [ Box 3 ]

After moving Box 2:
 [ Box 1 ]
       [ Box 2 ]
 [ Box 3 ]
 Box 2 moves, but its original space remains reserved

position: absolute 
 An absolutely positioned element is positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor
Example:
 .parent {
     position: relative;
 }
.child {
    position: absolute;
    top: 10px;
    right: 20px;
 }

Here: 
 .parent
    ↓
 position: relative 
    ↓
 .child
    ↓
 position: absolute

Important Point
 An absolutely positioned element is removed from the normal document flow
Therefore, other elements behave as if the absolute element is not occupying its original space.

Common Pattern
 .parent {
     position: relative;
 }

  .child {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
 }

 This is commonly used to position an element inside a container

position: fixed 
 A fixed element is positioned relative to the viewport
 It stays in the same place even when the page is scrolled

Example:
 .box { 
    position: fixed;
    top: 10px;
    right: 20px;
 }

When the user scrolls:

 Page scrolls ↓

 Fixed element
      ↓
 Stays in the same screen position

Common Uses
 Fixed navigation bars
 Floating buttons
 Fixed headers
 Chat buttons

position: sticky 
 sticky is a combination of relative-like behavior and fixed-like behavior
 The element behaves normally until a specified scrolling position is reached. Then it sticks to that position within its scrolling/container context
Example:
 h2 {
     position: sticky;
     top: 0;
 }
When scrolling:
 Before reaching top:
 Heading
    ↓
 Normal position

After reaching top:
 Heading
 ────────────
 Sticks while its container allows

Common Uses
 Sticky headings
 Sticky navigation
 Section headers

sticky → normal first → sticks while scrolling

top, right, bottom, left 
 These properties are used to control the position of positioned elements.
Example:
 .box {
     position: relative;
     top: 20px;
     left: 30px;
 }
Meaning:
 top: 20px
 → move downward

 left: 30px
 → move toward the right

Other properties:
 right: 20px;
 bottom: 20px;

These properties are useful with:
 relative
 absolute
 fixed
 sticky

z-index 
 z-index controls the stacking order of overlapping elements.
 A higher z-index generally places an element in front of another overlapping element.
Example:
 .box1 {
     position: relative;
     z-index: 1;
 }
 .box2 {
     position: relative;
     z-index: 2;
 }
Here:
 box2
   ↓
 higher z-index
   ↓
 appears above box1

z-index becomes useful when elements overlap

Example:
        Box 2
      ┌────────┐
      │        │
   ┌──┼────────┼──┐
   │  │  Box 1 │  │
   └──┴────────┴──┘
 If box2 has a higher stacking order, it can appear in front

 z-index → controls front/back order

Position Comparison 
 z-index controls the stacking order of overlapping elements.

A higher z-index generally places an element in front of another overlapping element.

Example:

.box1 {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
}

.box2 {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
}

Here:

box2
  ↓
higher z-index
  ↓
appears above box1
Important

z-index becomes useful when elements overlap.

Example:

        Box 2
      ┌────────┐
      │        │
   ┌──┼────────┼──┐
   │  │  Box 1 │  │
   └──┴────────┴──┘

If box2 has a higher stacking order, it can appear in front.

Memory Trick:
z-index → controls front/back order

Position Comparison 
 Position   	Reference / Behavior	                     Stays during scroll
 static	      Normal document flow	                     No
 relative	    Original position                     	   No
 absolute	    Nearest positioned ancestor                No
 fixed	      Viewport                                   yes 
 sticky	      Normal until threshold, then sticks	       Yes, within its context

Relative vs Absolute 
 Relative
 .box {
     position: relative;
     left: 30px;
 }

 Original position
       ↓
     [BOX]
       ↓
 moves from its original position
       ↓
 original space remains

Absolute
 .box { 
    position: absolute;
    left: 30px;
 }
 Removed from normal flow
       ↓
 Positioned relative to nearest positioned ancestor

Relative → moves from its own original position
Absolute → positioned relative to positioned ancestor

CSS Variable 
 CSS Variables are custom properties used to store values that can be reused throughout CSS 
 They are useful when the same value, such as a color, is used many times 

Example:
 :root {
  --primary-color: blue;
 }

Here:
 --primary-color -> variable name 
   blue          -> Variable value 

Why Use Css Variable?
 Suppose a website uses the same color many times:
 nav {
  background-color: blue;
 }

 .button {
  background-color: blue;
 }

 .heading {
    color: blue;
 }

If we want to change blue to red, we would have to change it in multiple places.
With a CSS variable:

 :root {
    --primary-color: blue;
 }

Use it:
 nav {
     background-color: var(--primary-color);
 }

 .button {
    background-color: var(--primary-color);
}

Now changing:

 --primary-color: red;

changes all places using that variable

 Main Benefit
 One variable
      ↓
 Used in many places
      ↓
 Change once
      ↓
 All related styles update

Declaring a CSS Variable 
 CSS variables are declared using two hyphens --

Syntax:
 --variable-name: value;
Example:
 :root {
    --primary-color: blue;
    --secondary-color: lightblue;
 }

Using a CSS Variable-- var()
 The var() function is used to access a CSS variable.

Syntax:
 var(--variable-name)

Example:
 :root {
     --primary-color: blue;
 }
 
 h1 {
     color: var(--primary-color);
 }

Here:

 --primary-color
        ↓
      blue
        ↓
 var(--primary-color)
        ↓
 color: blue

:root 
 :root represents the root element of the document

CSS variables declared inside :root are commonly used as global variables
Example:
 :root {
     --primary-color: blue;
     --secondary-color: lightblue;
 }

These variables can generally be used by elements throughout the document.

Global CSS Variable 
 A global variable can be used throughout the document.

Example:
 :root {
     --main-color: blue;
 }

 h1 {
     color: var(--main-color);
 }

 p {
     color: var(--main-color);
 }
 Both h1 and p can use the same variable

Local CSS Variable 
 A CSS variable can also be declared inside a specific selector.

Example:
 .box {
     --main-color: red;
 }

The variable is available to the element and its descendants within that scope.

Example:
 .box { 
     --main-color: red;
 }

 .box p {
     color: var(--main-color);
 }
Difference
 Global Variable
 → available throughout the document

 Local Variable
 → available within its scope

Local Variable Can Override Global Variable 
 A local variable can have the same name as a global variable 
 
Example:
 :root {
     --main-color: blue;
 }

 .box {
    --main-color: red;
 }

 .box p {
     color: var(--main-color);
 }

Here:
 Global --main-color → blue

Inside .box:
 --main-color → red

 So the paragraph inside .box uses:
 red

CSS Variable Inheritance 
 CSS custom properties can be inherited by child elements.

Example:
 .box {
     --main-color: blue;
 }

 .box p {
    color: var(--main-color);
 }

The child <p> can use the variable defined on its parent .box.

Think:
 Parent
   ↓
 CSS Variable
   ↓
 Child
   ↓
 Can inherit/use variable

Fallback Value 
 A fallback value can be provided inside var()

Syntax:
 var(--variable, fallback-value)

Example:
 h1 {
     color: var(--main-color, black);
 }

Meaning:
 If --main-color exists
        ↓
 Use --main-color

 Otherwise
         ↓
 Use black

Example
 color: var(--primary-color, blue);

Here:
 --primary-color → preferred value
 blue            → fallback value

CSS Variables can Store Different Values 
 CSS variables are not limited to colors

They can store values such as:
 :root {
     --main-color: blue;
     --opacity: 0.5;
     --font-size: 20px;
     --spacing: 10px;
 }

Use them:
 .box {
     color: var(--main-color);
     opacity: var(--opacity);
     font-size: var(--font-size);
     padding: var(--spacing);
 }

Benefits 
 Reusable values 
 Easier theme changes 
 Less repetition 
 Easier maintenance 
 Can have global or local scope 
 Can  be inherited by child elemments

Media Queries 
 Media Queries are used to apply different CSS styles depending on the characteristics of the device or screen
 They are mainly used to make websites responsive

Responsive website:

 Desktop
    ↓
 Tablet
    ↓
 Mobile

 The website automatically adjusts its layout and styling according to the screen size

Why Use Media Queries?
 Without media queries, a website designed for a large screen may not look good on a mobile screen
 Media queries allow us to change CSS for different screen sizes

Example:
 Large screen
     ↓
 Normal layout

 Small screen
     ↓
 Different layout

Basic Syntax 
 @media screen and (max-width: 800px)
 {
     /* CSS rules */
 }

Here:
 @media       → Starts the media query
 screen       → Media type
 max-width    → Condition
 800px        → Maximum screen width

 The CSS inside the media query is applied when the condition is true

max-width 
max-width means:
 Apply these styles when the  screen width is 800px or less 

Example:
 @media screen and (max-width: 800px)
 {
     body {
         background-color: lightblue;
     }
 }

So:
 Width ≤ 800px
       ↓
 Media query applies
       ↓
 Background becomes lightblue

min-width 
min-width means:
 Apply these styles when the screen width is at least the specified value.

Example:
 @media screen and (min-width: 800px)
 {
     body {
        background-color: lightgreen;
     }
 }

Changing Layout with Media Queries 
Suppose we have:

 .container {
     display: flex;
 }

For a smaller screen, we can change the layout:
 @media screen and (max-width: 600px)
 {
     .container {
         flex-direction: column;
     }
 }

So:
 Large screen
 → Horizontal layout

Small screen
 → Vertical layout

 This is one of the most common uses of media queries

Changing Font Size 
 Media queries can also change font sizes
Example:
 h1 {
     font-size: 40px;
 }

 @media screen and (max-width: 600px)
 {
     h1 {
         font-size: 25px;
     }
 }

Result:
 Large screen → 40px
 Small screen → 25px

Multiple Conditions 
 Media queries can contain multiple conditions.
Example:
 @media screen and (min-width: 600px) and (max-width: 1000px)
 {
     body {
         background-color: lightblue;
     }
 }

The CSS applies when:
 600px ≤ screen width ≤ 1000px

Screen 
 screen is a media type used for screens such as computers, tablets and phones.

Example:
 @media screen and (max-width: 600px)
 {
     /* CSS */
 }
 For basic responsive web development, screen is the media type you will commonly encounter
 */

Media Query Example 
 <!DOCTYPE html> 
 <html> 
 <head> 
 <style>
 body { 
    background-color: white; 
 } 

 h1 { 
      font-size: 40px; 
 } 
 @media screen and (max-width: 600px) 
 { 
    body { 
        background-color: lightblue;
       } 
        h1 { 
          font-size: 25px; 
    } 
 }
  </style>
  </head> 
  <body> 
  <h1>Hello World</h1>
  </body>
  </html>
 
Common Media Query Conditions 
Maximum width
 @media screen and (max-width: 600px)
 Used mainly to target smaller screens

Minimum width
 @media screen and (min-width: 600px)
 Used to target screens at or above a certain width

Media Queries and Responsive Design 
            Responsive Website 
                    ↓ 
               Media Queries 
                    ↓ 
       ┌────────────┼────────────┐ 
       ↓            ↓            ↓ 
       Desktop   Tablet        Mobile 
          ↓        ↓             ↓ 
        Different CSS styles when needed
 The goal is to make the same website adapt to different screen sizes rather than 
 creating completely separate websites

float 
 The CSS float property is used to move an element to the left or right of its containing element.

Syntax:
 selector {
     float: value;
 }

Main values:
 left
 right
 none

float: left 
 Moves an element to the left side.

Example:
 img {
     float: left;
 }

The content around the image can flow alongside it
 ┌─────────┐  Text Text Text
 │  Image  │  Text Text Text
 │         │  Text Text Text
 └─────────┘  Text Text Text

float: right  
 Moves an element to the right side.

Example:
 img {
     float: right;
 }

Text can flow around the left side of the floated element.
 Text Text Text  ┌─────────┐
 Text Text Text  │  Image  │
 Text Text Text  │         │
 Text Text Text  └─────────┘

float: none 
 none means the element does not float
 img {
     float: none;
 }
 none is the default value

Float with Text 
 One of the original purposes of float was to allow text to wrap around images, similar to layouts commonly seen in newspapers and blogs.

Example:
 <img src="image.jpg" alt="Example">

 <p>
    This is some text that can flow around the image
 </p>
 img {
     float: left;
     margin-right: 10px;
 }

The image moves left and the text flows around it

Problem with Float 
 Floated elements can sometimes cause the parent container to collapse or fail to contain the floated content properly

Example:
 Parent container
 ┌────────────────────────┐
 │                        │
 └────────────────────────┘

     Float
       ↓
    ┌──────┐
    │Image │
    └──────┘
The parent may not automatically account for the floated child's height in the way you expect

display: flow-root 
 flow-root can be used to create a new block formatting context and help the parent properly contain floated children.

Example:
 .parent {
     display: flow-root;
 }

Think:
 Parent
  d  ↓
 display: flow-root
   ↓
 Contains floated children

clear 
 The clear property controls whether an element can appear beside floated elements.

Syntax:
 selector {
     clear: value;
 }

Main values:
 left
 right
 both
 none

clear: left 
 Prevents the element from appearing beside a left-floated element

Example:
 .box {
     clear: left;
 }
 The element moves below the left-floated content when necessary

clear: right 
 Prevents the element from appearing beside a right-floated element

 .box {
     clear: right;
 }

clear: both 
 Prevents the element from appearing beside both left- and right-floated elements

Example:
 .footer {
    clear: both;
 }
This is commonly used when you want the next section to start below all floated elements

clear: none 
 Allows the element to appear beside floated elements

 .box {
     clear: none;
 }
This is the default behavior

Float vs Clear
 Property	   Purpose
 float	      Moves an element left or right
 clear	      Controls whether an element can appear beside floats

Float vs Modern Layout
 float is important to understand, especially for older websites and for understanding existing CSS
 However, Flexbox and CSS Grid are generally better suited for creating modern page layouts

Use:
 Float
  ↓
 Mainly text wrapping / legacy layouts

 Flexbox / Grid
  ↓
 Modern page layouts
 
Flexbox
 Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout) is a CSS layout system used to arrange elements in a row or column.

It makes it easier to:
 Arrange elements
 Align elements
 Create responsive layouts
 Control spacing between elements

To use Flexbox, make an element a flex container:
 .container {
     display: flex;
 }

The children of .container become flex items
 .container
     ↓
 display: flex
     ↓
 ┌──────┬──────┬──────┐
 │ Item │ Item │ Item │
 └──────┴──────┴──────┘

Flex Container and Flex Items 
 The element with:
 display: flex;
 is called the flex container.

 Its direct children are called flex items

Example:
 <div class="container">
    <div>Item 1</div>
    <div>Item 2</div>
    <div>Item 3</div>
 </div>
 .container {
     display: flex;
 }

Here:
 .container → Flex Container
 Item 1
 Item 2
 Item 3
     ↓
 Flex Items

Main Axis and Cross Axis 
 Flexbox works using two axes:

Main Axis
    →
    ─────────────────

Cross Axis
    ↑
    │
    │
    ↓

By default:
 flex-direction: row;

So:
 Main Axis   → Horizontal
 Cross Axis  ↓ Vertical

justify-content 
 justify-content controls the alignment of flex items along the main axis.

Example:
 .container {
     display: flex;
     justify-content: center;
 }

Common values:
 flex-start
 flex-end
 center
 space-between
 space-around
 space-evenly

flex-start
 Items move toward the start
 [1][2][3]────────

flex-end
 Items move toward the end
 ────────[1][2][3]

center
 Items are centered
 ────[1][2][3]────

space-between
 Equal space is placed between the items
 [1]────[2]────[3]

space-around
 Space is placed around each item

space-evenly
 Equal space is placed between items and at the edges

align-items 
 align-items controls alignment along the cross axis

Example:
 .container {
     display: flex;
     align-items: center;
 }

Common values:
 stretch
 flex-start
 flex-end
 center

For the default row direction:
 justify-content → Horizontal
 align-items     → Vertical

Important
 Don't memorize this as permanently horizontal/vertical

Remember:
 justify-content → Main Axis
 align-items     → Cross Axis
 The direction can change with flex-direction

flex-direction 
 flex-direction controls the direction in which flex items are arranged

Row
 flex-direction: row;

Default:
 [1] [2] [3] →

Row Reverse
 flex-direction: row-reverse;
 ← [3] [2] [1]

Column
 flex-direction: column;
 [1]
  ↓
 [2]
  ↓
 [3]

Column Reverse
 flex-direction: column-reverse;
 [3]
  ↑
 [2]
  ↑
 [1]

Flex Axis Changess with Direction 
 This is important
For:
 flex-direction: row;
 Main Axis  → Horizontal
 Cross Axis ↓ Vertical

For:
 flex-direction: column;
 Main Axis  ↓ Vertical
 Cross Axis → Horizontal

Therefore:
 justify-content works on the main axis, and align-items works on the cross axis

flex-wrap 
 By default, flex items try to stay on one line.

flex-wrap allows items to move to another line when there isn't enough space.

.container {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
}

Without wrapping:

[1][2][3][4][5][6]

With wrapping:

[1][2][3][4]
[5][6]

Common values:

nowrap
wrap
wrap-reverse

Default:

flex-wrap: nowrap;

wrap-reverse 
 flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;
 Items can wrap onto multiple lines, but the cross-axis direction of the lines is reversed

align-content 
 align-content controls the alignment of multiple flex lines along the cross axis.

It becomes useful when:
 flex-wrap: wrap;
 creates multiple rows/columns

Example:
 .container {
     display: flex;
     flex-wrap: wrap;
     align-content: center;
 }

Important Difference
align-items
 → Aligns items within a flex line

align-content
 → Aligns multiple flex lines

flex-flow 
 flex-flow is a shorthand for:
 flex-direction
 +
 flex-wrap

Example:
 .container {
     flex-flow: row wrap;
 }

This is equivalent to:
 .container {
     flex-direction: row;
     flex-wrap: wrap;
 }

gap 
 gap creates space between flex items.

Example:
 .container {
     display: flex;
     gap: 20px;
 }

Result:
 [1]  20px  [2]  20px  [3]

You can also use:
 row-gap: 10px;
 column-gap: 20px;

Common Flexbox Mistake 
Remember that:
 justify-content
and:
 align-items
 depend on the flex direction

Don't simply memorize:
 justify = horizontal
 align = vertical

Instead remember:
 justify-content → Main Axis
 align-items     → Cross Axis

order 
 order controls the visual order of individual flex items.

Example:
 .item1 {
     order: 2;
 }

 .item2 {
     order: 1;
 }

Then item2 appears before item1

Default:
 order: 0;

Smaller order values appear earlier

Example:
 order: 1
 order: 2
 order: 3

appears as:
 1 → 2 → 3

flex-grow 
 flex-grow controls how much a flex item can grow when extra space is available.

Example:
 .item1 {
     flex-grow: 1;
 }

If two items have:
 .item1 {
     flex-grow: 1;
 }

 .item2 {
     flex-grow: 2;
 }

 then, under the same relevant conditions, item 2 receives twice the growth share of item 1

flex-shrink 
 flex-shrink controls how much a flex item can shrink when there isn't enough space.

Example:
 .item1 {
     flex-shrink: 1;
 }

Default:
 flex-shrink: 1;

align-self 
 align-self allows one individual flex item to have its own cross-axis alignment.

Example:
 .item1 {
     align-self: flex-end;
 }

While the container might have:
 align-items: center;
 item1 can override that alignment for itself

align-items 
 → controls all flex items 

align-self 
 → controls one flex item

Important Flexbox Properties 
 Container Properties
 display
 flex-direction
 flex-wrap
 flex-flow
 justify-content
 align-items
 align-content
 gap

Item Properties
 order
 flex-grow
 flex-shrink
 align-self

Grid  
 CSS Grid is a CSS layout system used to arrange elements in rows and columns
 It is especially useful for creating two-dimensional layouts

To create a grid:
 .container {
     display: grid;
 }

Example:
 ┌────────┬────────┬────────┐
 │ Item 1 │ Item 2 │ Item 3 │
 ├────────┼────────┼────────┤
 │ Item 4 │ Item 5 │ Item 6 │
 └────────┴────────┴────────┘

Flexbox vs Grid
Flexbox → Mainly one-dimensional
           Row OR Column

Grid    → Two-dimensional
           Rows AND Columns

grid-template-columns 
 grid-template-columns defines the number and size of columns

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     grid-template-columns: 100px 200px 100px;
 }

This creates:
 Column 1 → 100px
 Column 2 → 200px
 Column 3 → 100px

Example:
 ┌───────┬────────────┬───────┐
 │       │            │       │
 │ 100px │    200px   │ 100px │
 │       │            │       │
 └───────┴────────────┴───────┘

Grid Lines 
 Grid lines are the lines that divide the grid into rows and columns

Example:

 1       2       3       4
 │       │       │       │
 ├───────┼───────┼───────┤
 │       │       │       │
 ├───────┼───────┼───────┤
 │       │       │       │

 For 3 columns, there are 4 vertical grid lines
 3 columns → 4 column lines

Grid Cell 
 A grid cell is a single unit/box in the grid.

Example:
 ┌────────┬────────┐
 │ Cell 1 │ Cell 2 │
 ├────────┼────────┤
 │ Cell 3 │ Cell 4 │
 └────────┴────────┘

 Each box is a grid cell

Grid Track 
 A grid track is the space between two adjacent grid lines.

There are two types:
 Column Track → vertical space
 Row Track    → horizontal space

Example:
 ┌────────┬────────┐
 │        │        │
 │        │        │
 └────────┴────────┘
    ↑
 Column Track

Grid Area 
 A grid area is a rectangular area of one or more grid cells.

Example:
 ┌────────┬────────┐
 │        │        │
 │  Area  │  Area  │
 ├────────┼────────┤
 │        │        │
 └────────┴────────┘
 A grid area can contain one or multiple cells

grid-row 
 grid-row controls where a grid item starts and ends across rows

Example:
 .item {
     grid-row: 1 / 3;
 }

 This means the item starts at row line 1 and ends at row line 3
 Therefore, it occupies:
 Row 1
 Row 2

grid-column 
 grid-column controls where a grid item starts and ends across columns.

Example:
 .item {
     grid-column: 1 / 3;
 }

 The item occupies columns between line 1 and line 3
 So it spans 2 columns

Example:
 ┌──────────────────┬────────┐
 │      Item        │        │
 │   spans 2 cols   │ Item   │
 └──────────────────┴────────┘

grid-row + grid-column 
 We can control both dimensions

 .item {
     grid-row: 1 / 3;
     grid-column: 1 / 3;
 }

 The item spans:
 2 rows × 2 columns

Naming Grid Lines 
 Grid lines can be given names.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     grid-template-columns:
         [start] 100px
         [middle] 100px
         [end] 100px;
 }

Now the lines have names:
 [start]    [middle]    [end]
    │          │           │
    ├──────────┼───────────┤

 Named lines can then be used when positioning grid items

grid-template-areas 
 grid-template-areas allows us to create a layout by giving names to different areas.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;

     grid-template-areas:
         "header header"
         "sidebar main"
         "footer footer";
 }

 This creates a layout like:
 ┌───────────────┐
 │     header    │
 ├───────┬───────┤
 │sidebar│ main  │
 ├───────┴───────┤
 │     footer    │
 └───────────────┘

 Then assign areas to elements:

 .header {
     grid-area: header;
 }

 .sidebar {
     grid-area: sidebar;
 }

 .main {
     grid-area: main;
 }

 .footer {
     grid-area: footer;
 }

 This is very useful for webpage layouts

fr Unit 
 fr means fraction of the available space in the grid container.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
 }

The available space is divided equally:
 ┌────────────┬────────────┐
 │    1fr     │    1fr     │
 │    50%     │    50%     │
 └────────────┴────────────┘

Example:
 grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;
 The available space is divided into 3 parts:
 1fr → 1 part
 2fr → 2 parts

So approximately:
 ┌───────┬───────────────┐
 │  1fr  │      2fr      │
 └───────┴───────────────┘

gap 
 gap creates space between grid rows and columns.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     gap: 20px;
 }

 There will be 20px space between grid items
 You can also use:
 row-gap: 10px;
 column-gap: 20px;

align-items 
 align-items controls the alignment of grid items along the block/cross axis within their grid areas.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     align-items: center;
 }

Common values:
 start
 center
 end
 stretch

justify-items 
 justify-items controls the alignment of grid items along the inline axis within their grid areas

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     justify-items: center;
 }
 This can center the items inside their grid cells/areas

repeat() Function 
 repeat() makes repeated grid tracks easier to write.

Instead of:
 grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;

we can write:
 grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);

Meaning:
 4 columns
 each = 1fr

Another example:
 grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 100px);

Meaning:
 100px 100px 100px

Syntax
 repeat(number, value)

Justify-content 
 justify-content controls the position of the entire grid inside its container when there is extra space available.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     justify-content: center;
 }

The whole grid is centered inside the container
Important

Don't confuse:
justify-items
 → positions items inside their grid areas

justify-content
 → positions the whole grid inside the container

align-content 
 align-content controls the position of the entire grid along the other axis when there is extra space available.

Example:
 .container {
     display: grid;
     align-content: center;
 }
Difference
align-items
 → items inside their grid areas

align-content
 → entire grid inside the container

Grid Properties 
Container Properties
 display: grid
 grid-template-columns
 grid-template-rows
 grid-template-areas
 gap
 row-gap
 column-gap
 align-items
 justify-items
 align-content
 justify-content

Item Properties
 grid-row
 grid-column
 grid-area

Example Grid Layout 
 .container { 
  display: grid; 
  
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 1fr; 
  gap: 10px; 
  grid-template-areas: 
  "header header header" 
  "sidebar main main" 
  "footer footer footer"; 
  } 
  .header { 
    grid-area: header; 
  } 
  .sidebar { 
      grid-area: sidebar; 
  } 
  .main { 
       grid-area: main; 
  } 
  .footer { 
      grid-area: footer; 
 }

Layout:

 ┌─────────────────────────────┐
 │           HEADER            │
 ├──────────┬──────────────────┤
 │ SIDEBAR  │       MAIN       │
 ├──────────┴──────────────────┤
 │           FOOTER            │
 └─────────────────────────────┘

CSS Transform 
 The transform property is used to visually modify an element by:
 Rotating it
 Scaling it
 Moving it
 Skewing it

Syntax:
 .element {
     transform: transform-function();
 }

Example:
 .box {
     transform: rotate(45deg);
 }

rotate()
 rotate() rotates an element around its transform origin.

Example:
 .box {
     transform: rotate(45deg);
 }

 The element rotates by 45deg

Example:
 transform: rotate(90deg);

Negative value
 transform: rotate(-45deg);
 Rotates in the opposite direction

scale()
 scale() changes the size of an element.

Example:
 .box {
     transform: scale(2);
 }

 This makes the element approximately 2 times larger

Example:
 transform: scale(0.5);
 This makes the element smaller

You can also specify different horizontal and vertical scaling:
 transform: scale(2, 1);

skew()
 skew() tilts or slants an element.

Example:
 .box {
     transform: skew(20deg);
 }

You can also specify both axes:
 transform: skew(20deg, 10deg);

translate()
 translate() moves an element from its current position.

Example:
 .box {
     transform: translate(50px, 20px);
 }

Here:
 50px → Horizontal movement
 20px → Vertical movement

You can also use:
 transform: translateX(50px);
or:
 transform: translateY(20px);

X and Y Transform Functions 
translateX()
 Moves an element horizontally
 transform: translateX(50px);

translateY()
 Moves an element vertically
 transform: translateY(50px);

scaleX()
 Scales horizontally
 transform: scaleX(2);

scaleY()
 Scales vertically
 transform: scaleY(2);

Using Multiple Transform functions 
 Multiple transformations can be applied to the same element

Example:
 .box {
     transform: rotate(45deg) scale(1.5) translateX(20px);
 }

 Here the element is:

 Rotated
    ↓
 Scaled
    ↓
 Translated
 Multiple transform functions can be written in the same transform declaration

Transform Origin 
 transform-origin specifies the point around which a transformation happens
 By default, the transformation origin is generally the center of the element

Example:
 .box {
     transform-origin: top left;
     transform: rotate(45deg);
 }

Now the element rotates around its:
 top-left corner

Other examples:
 transform-origin: center;
 transform-origin: top;
 transform-origin: bottom;
 transform-origin: left;
 transform-origin: right;

 You can also use percentages or lengths

Example:
 transform-origin: 0 0;

Why is transform Useful?
 Transforms are useful for creating:

 Rotating elements
 Hover effects
 Buttons
 Cards
 Animations
 Interactive UI elements
 Visual effects

Example:
 button:hover {
     transform: scale(1.1);
 }

When the mouse moves over the button:
 Normal button
       ↓
 Hover
      ↓
 Button becomes slightly larger

Transform vs Position 
 transform: translate() can visually move an element without changing the normal document flow in the same way as layout properties

Example:
 .box {
     transform: translateX(50px);
 }

This visually moves the box

Don't confuse:
 translate() → Transformation
 position     → Positioning/layout behavior

Common Transform Functions 
 Function	    Purpose
 rotate()	    Rotates element
 scale()	    Changes size
 skew()	      Tilts/slants element
 translate()	Moves element
 translateX()	Moves horizontally
 translateY()	Moves vertically
 scaleX()	    Scales horizontally
 scaleY()	    Scales vertically

Example 
 <div class="box">
    Hello
 </div>
 .box {
     width: 100px;
     height: 100px;
     background-color: lightblue;

     transform: rotate(20deg) scale(1.2);
 }

The element is:
 Rotate → 20deg
 Scale  → 1.2 times

Transition 
 A CSS transition makes a change from one CSS state to another happen smoothly instead of instantly.

Example:
 .box {
     background-color: blue;
     transition: background-color 1s;
 }

 .box:hover {
     background-color: red;
 }

Without transition:
 Blue → Red
 Instantly

With transition:
 Blue → gradually → Red

Why Do we Need Transitions?
 Suppose an element moves when we apply a class or :hover

Without transition:
 Position 1 ─────────→ Position 2
           sudden

With transition:
 Position 1 ──→ ──→ ──→ Position 2
               smooth

 Transitions make interfaces feel smoother and more interactive

Four Main Transition Properties
 CSS transitions can be controlled using:

 transition-property
 transition-duration
 transition-timing-function
 transition-delay
  Transition
      ↓
   ┌───────────────┬───────────────┐
   ↓               ↓               ↓
  Property      Duration        Timing Function
                          +
                        Delay

transition-property 
 transition-property specifies which CSS property should transition.

Example:
 .box {
     transition-property: background-color;
 }

This means:
 background-color
       ↓
 will change smoothly

Example:
 .box {
     transition-property: transform;
 }
 Only the transform property is transitioned

transition-duration 
 transition-duration specifies how long the transition takes.

Example:
 .box {
     transition-property: transform;
     transition-duration: 2s;
 }

The transformation takes:
 2 seconds

Example
 transition-duration: 500ms;

means:
 500 milliseconds

transition-timinng-function 
 transition-timing-function controls the speed pattern of the transition.

Example:
 .box {
     transition-timing-function: ease-in-out;
 }

Common values:
 linear
 ease
 ease-in
 ease-out
 ease-in-out

linear
 The transition progresses at a constant speed
 transition-timing-function: linear;

ease-in
 Starts slowly and becomes faster
 transition-timing-function: ease-in;

ease-out
 Starts faster and slows down
 transition-timing-function: ease-out;

ease-in-out
 Starts slowly, becomes faster, then slows down again
 transition-timing-function: ease-in-out;

transition-delay 
 transition-delay specifies how long to wait before the transition starts.

Example:
 .box {
     transition-delay: 1s;
 }

Meaning:
 State changes
       ↓
 Wait 1 second
       ↓
 Transition starts

Commbining All Transition Properties 
Example:
 .box {
     transition-property: transform;
     transition-duration: 2s;
     transition-timing-function: ease-in-out;
     transition-delay: 1s;
 }

Meaning:
 Property → transform
 Duration → 2 seconds
 Timing   → ease-in-out
 Delay    → 1 second

Transition on Multiple Properties 
 You can transition multiple CSS properties.

Example:
 .box {
     transition-property: background-color, transform;
     transition-duration: 1s, 2s;
 }

Here:
 background-color → 1 second
 transform        → 2 seconds
 Multiple properties can be specified using commas

transition: all
 You can transition all transitionable properties

Example:
 .box {
     transition: all 1s;
 }

 Now multiple changes can transition smoothly
 However, in real projects, it is often better to specify the properties you actually want to animate rather than blindly using all

Timing Functions 
 The timing function controls the speed curve of the transition.

Example:
 .box {
     transition: transform 2s ease-in-out;
 }

Common timing functions:
 linear
 ease
 ease-in
 ease-out
 ease-in-out

cubic-bezier()
 CSS also allows custom timing functions using cubic-bezier().

Example:
 .box {
     transition-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
 }
 This allows more precise control over how the transition changes speed

Transition Shorthand 
 Instead of writing all properties separately, we can use the transition shorthand property.

Example:
 .box {
     transition: transform 2s ease-in-out 1s;
 }

General form:
 transition:
 property
 duration
 timing-function
 delay;

So:
 transition: transform 2s ease-in-out 1s;

means:
 transform
    ↓
 2s duration
    ↓
 ease-in-out timing
    ↓
 1s delay

Multiple Transitions Usinng Shorthand 
 Multiple transitions can also be written using commas

Example:
.box {
    transition:
        background-color 1s,
        transform 2s;
}

Here:
 background-color → 1s
 transform        → 2s

Transition with transform 
 Transitions are commonly combined with the transform property.

Example:
 .box {
     transition: transform 1s;
 }

 .box:hover {
     transform: translateX(100px);
 }

When the mouse moves over the box:
 Normal
   ↓
 Hover
   ↓
 translateX(100px)
   ↓
 Smooth movement

This combines the concepts from Transform + Transition

Transition vs Transform 
 These two properties have different jobs.

Transform
 Changes the visual state:

 transform: scale(1.2);

Transition
 Controls how smoothly the change happens:
 transition: transform 1s;

Together:
 .box {
     transition: transform 1s;
 }

 .box:hover {
     transform: scale(1.2);
 }

Animation 
 CSS Animation allows an element to change from one style to another over a period of time
 Unlike a simple transition, an animation can have multiple stages/keyframes and can repeat automatically

 Animation
     ↓
 Multiple style changes
     ↓
 Over time
     ↓
 Can repeat automatically

@keyframes 
 @keyframes defines what happens during an animation.

Example:
 @keyframes moveBox
 {
     from {
         transform: translateX(0);
     }

     to {
         transform: translateX(300px);
     }
 }

Here:
 from → Starting state
 to   → Ending state

Applying Animation to an Element 
 After creating keyframes, use animation-name to apply them

 .box {
     animation-name: moveBox;
 }

Example:
 .box {
     animation-name: moveBox;
     animation-duration: 3s;
 }

Meaning:
 moveBox
    ↓
 Runs on .box
    ↓
 For 3 seconds

animation-duration 
 animation-duration specifies how long one complete animation cycle takes.

Example:
 .box {
     animation-duration: 3s;
 }

Meaning:
 One animation cycle = 3 seconds
 You can also use milliseconds:
 animation-duration: 500ms;

animation-iteration-count 
 This property specifies how many times the animation should repeat.

Example:
 .box {
     animation-iteration-count: 3;
 }

 The animation runs:
 1 → 2 → 3

For continuous animation:
 animation-iteration-count: infinite;

Remember
 3        → Run 3 times
 infinite → Keep repeating

animation-timing-function 
 Controls how the animation changes speed over time.

Common values:
 linear
 ease
 ease-in
 ease-out
 ease-in-out

Example:
 .box {
     animation-timing-function: ease-in-out;
 }

Meaning
 linear      → Constant speed
 ease-in     → Starts slowly
 ease-out    → Ends slowly
 ease-in-out → Slow → Fast → Slow

animation-delay 
 animation-delay specifies how long to wait before the animation starts.

Example:
 .box {
     animation-delay: 2s;
 }

Meaning:
 Page loads
    ↓
 Wait 2 seconds
    ↓
 Animation starts

Difference
 animation-duration → How long animation runs
 animation-delay    → How long before animation starts

animation-direction 
 Controls the direction in which the animation plays.

Common values:
 normal
 reverse
 alternate
 alternate-reverse
 normal

Animation plays normally:
 Start → End
 reverse

Animation plays backwards:
 End → Start
 alternate

Animation changes direction after each cycle:
 Start → End
 End → Start
 Start → End
 alternate-reverse

 Starts in reverse and then alternates

animation-play-state 
 Controls whether an animation is running or paused.

Example:
 .box {
     animation-play-state: paused;
 }

Common values:
 running
 paused

Example:
 .box:hover {
     animation-play-state: paused;
 }

 The animation pauses when the mouse is over the element

animation-fill-mode 
 Controls the styles applied to an element before and/or after the animation.

Common values:
 none
 forwards
 backwards
 both

none
 Default behavior
 The element does not retain animation styles before/after the animation.

forwards
 The element keeps the styles from the final keyframe after the animation finishes.
 animation-fill-mode: forwards;

backwards
 The element uses the styles from the first relevant keyframe during the delay.

both
 Combines the behavior of:
 backwards + forwards
 
Animation Using Percentages 
 Instead of only using:
 from
 to
 we can define multiple stages using percentages

Example:
 @keyframes moveBox
 {
     0% {
         transform: translateX(0);
     }

     25% {
         transform: translateX(100px);
     }

     50% {
         transform: translateX(200px);
     }
 
     100% {
         transform: translateX(300px);
     }
 }

This allows us to create more complex animations

 0%
  ↓
 25%
  ↓
 50%
  ↓
 100%

Complete Animation Example 
  @keyframes moveBox
 {
     from {
         transform: translateX(0);
     }
 
     to {
         transform: translateX(300px);
     }
 }

 .box {
     animation-name: moveBox;
     animation-duration: 3s;
     animation-iteration-count: infinite;
 }

Here:
 @keyframes
      ↓
 Define animation

 animation-name
     ↓
 Choose animation

 animation-duration
      ↓
 Set duration

 animation-iteration-count
      ↓
 Set repetitions

Animation Shorthand 
 Instead of writing every property separately, we can use the animation shorthand.

Example:
 .box {
     animation: moveBox 3s ease-in-out 1s infinite;
 }

It can include:
 name
 duration
 timing-function
 delay
 iteration-count
 direction
 fill-mode
 play-state

 don't need to memorize every shorthand order immediately. The individual properties are easier to understand first

Multiple Animations 
 More than one animation can be applied to the same element.

Use commas:
 .box {
     animation:
         moveBox 3s ease-in-out infinite,
         changeColor 2s linear infinite;
 }

 Here two animations run on the same element:
 moveBox
    +
 changeColor
    ↓
 Same element

 Each animation can have its own properties

Animation vs Transition 
Transition
 Best for a relatively simple change between states.

Example:
 .box {
     transition: transform 1s;
 }

 .box:hover {
    transform: scale(1.2);
 }

Animation
Useful when you need:
 Multiple stages
 Automatic repetition
 Delays
 Direction control
 Keyframes
 More complex sequences

Example:
 @keyframes moveBox
 {
     0% {
         transform: translateX(0);
     }

     50% {
         transform: translateX(200px);
     }

     100% {
         transform: translateX(0);
     }
 }

Easy Difference
 Transition
     ↓
 State A → State B

 Animation
     ↓
 Keyframes
     ↓
 Multiple stages
     ↓
 Can repeat automatically

Why do we need object-fit?
 When an image or video is placed inside a fixed-size container, its original aspect ratio may not match the container
 This can cause the image to look:
 Stretched
 Squashed
 Cropped

 object-fit controls how replaced elements like <img> and <video> fit inside their container

Example:
 img {
     width: 300px;
     height: 200px;
     object-fit: cover;
 }

object-fit 
 The object-fit property controls how an image or video fits inside its given width and height.

Common values:
 fill
 contain
 cover
 none
 scale-down

object-fit: fill 
 img {
     object-fit: fill;
 }

 The image is resized to completely fill the container
 It may become distorted if the container and image have different aspect ratios

 Image
   ↓
 Forced to fit
   ↓
 May stretch/squash

object-fit: contain 
 img {
     object-fit: contain;
 }

 The entire image is visible while maintaining its aspect ratio

 Empty space may remain inside the container.

 ┌─────────────────────┐
 │                     │
 │      ┌────────┐     │
 │      │ Image  │     │
 │      └────────┘     │
 │                     │
 └─────────────────────┘

object-fit: cover 
 img {
     object-fit: cover;
 }

 The image fills the entire container while maintaining its aspect ratio
 Some part of the image may be cropped

 ┌─────────────────────┐
 │█████████████████████│
 │████   IMAGE   ██████│
 │█████████████████████│
 └─────────────────────┘

object-fit: none 
  img {
     object-fit: none;
 }

 The replaced element keeps its original size rather than being resized to fit the container
 Parts of the image may therefore be outside the container

object-fit: scale-down 
 img {
     object-fit: scale-down;
 }
 The browser chooses whichever results in the smaller rendered object size, effectively behaving like none or contain, whichever is smaller 

object-position 
 object-position controls which part of the image is positioned inside the container
 It is especially useful with:
 object-fit: cover;

Example:
 img {
     width: 300px;
     height: 200px;

     object-fit: cover;
     object-position: center;
 }

Common object-position values 
 object-position: center;
 object-position: top;
 object-position: bottom;
 object-position: left;
 object-position: right;

Example:
 img {
     object-fit: cover;
     object-position: top;
 }
 This keeps the top part of the image positioned in the container when cropping occurs

Percentage / Length Values 
 object-position can also use percentages or lengths.

Example:
 img {
     object-position: 50% 50%;
 }

Here:
 50% → Horizontal position
 50% → Vertical position

Another example:
 object-position: 0% 0%;
 means the object is positioned at the top-left

Complete Example 
 <img src="image.jpg" class="photo">
 .photo {
     width: 300px;
     height: 200px;
 
     object-fit: cover;
     object-position: center;
 }

Meaning:
 width + height
       ↓
 Fixed container
       ↓
 object-fit: cover
       ↓
 Image covers container
       ↓
 object-position: center
       ↓
 Center part is shown

background-image 
 CSS can also be used to set an image as the background of an element.

Example:
 .box {
    background-image: url("image.jpg");
 }

 The image becomes the background of .box

background-size 
 background-size controls the size of a background image.

cover
 .box {
     background-size: cover;
 }

 The background image covers the entire element

 Some parts may be cropped

contain
 .box {
     background-size: contain;
 }

 The complete background image is visible, but empty space may remain

background-position 
 Controls the position of the background image.

Example:
 .box {
     background-position: center;
 }

Other values:
 background-position: top;
 background-position: bottom;
 background-position: left;
 background-position: right;

background-repeat 
 Controls whether the background image repeats.

Example:
 .box {
     background-repeat: no-repeat;
 }

Common values:
 repeat
 no-repeat
 repeat-x
 repeat-y
 Meaning
 repeat
 → Repeat in both directions

no-repeat
 → Do not repeat

repeat-x
 → Repeat horizontally

repeat-y
 → Repeat vertically

Background Shorthand 
 Multiple background properties can be written using the background shorthand

Example:
 .box {
     background: url("image.jpg") center / cover no-repeat;
 }
 This combines background settings into one declaration

object-fit vs background-size 
 Both can be used to control how images fit into a container, but they are used differently

 <img> / <video>

Use:
 object-fit: cover;
 CSS background image

Use:
 background-size: cover;

Filters
 CSS filter is used to apply visual effects to an element, especially images

Syntax:
 img {
     filter: filter-function(value);
 }

Example:
 img {
     filter: blur(5px);
 }

blur()
 Makes an element look blurred

 img {
     filter: blur(5px);
 }

 The larger the value, the more blurred the image becomes

 blur(0px) → No blur
 blur(5px) → Slight blur
 blur(10px) → More blur

brightness()
 Controls the brightness of an element

 img {
     filter: brightness(50%);
 }

Examples:
 filter: brightness(50%);
 filter: brightness(100%);
 filter: brightness(150%);
 < 100% → Darker
 100%   → Normal
 > 100% → Brighter

contrast() 
 Controls the contrast of an element

 img {
     filter: contrast(150%);
 }

Examples:
 filter: contrast(50%);
 filter: contrast(100%);
 filter: contrast(150%);
 < 100% → Lower contrast
 100%   → Normal
 > 100% → Higher contrast

grayscale()
 Converts an element into grayscale

 img {
     filter: grayscale(100%);
 }

Examples:
 filter: grayscale(0%);
 filter: grayscale(50%);
 filter: grayscale(100%);
 0%   → Original colors
 50%  → Partially grayscale
 100% → Completely grayscale

sepia()
 Applies a sepia/brownish vintage effect

 img {
     filter: sepia(100%);
 }

Examples:
 filter: sepia(50%);
 filter: sepia(100%);

 100% gives the maximum sepia effect

invert()
 Inverts the colors of an element
 img {
     filter: invert(100%);
 }

Example:
 Black → White
 White → Black

Common values:
 filter: invert(0%);
 filter: invert(100%);
 0%   → Normal colors
 100% → Fully inverted colors

opacity() 
 Controls the transparency of an element 
 img {
     filter: opacity(50%);
 }

Examples:
 filter: opacity(100%);
 filter: opacity(50%);
 filter: opacity(0%);
 100% → Fully visible
 50%  → Half transparent
 0%   → Fully transparent

Multiple Filters 
 Multiple filter functions can be applied to the same element

Example:
 img {
     filter: grayscale(100%) blur(2px);
 }

Another example:
 img {
     filter: brightness(120%) contrast(150%);
 }

 Filters are written one after another in the same filter property

Example 
 <img src="image.jpg" class="photo">
 .photo {
     width: 300px;
     filter: grayscale(100%);
 }

 The image will appear completely grayscale

Figma 
 Figma is a collaborative design tool used to create designs and prototypes for websites, apps, and other digital products
 It helps developers/designers visualize a website before writing HTML, CSS and JavaScript

Why is Figma useful?
 Plan the website design before coding
 Create UI layouts visually
 Share designs with others
 Collaborate with team members
 Get design assets for development
 Reduce misunderstanding between designer, developer and client

 Figma helps with the design part, while HTML/CSS/JavaScript are used to build the actual website

Figma Community 
 Figma has a Community section where users can find designs, templates, icons and UI resources created by other users
 You can search for things related to your project

Example:
 Search → Blog UI
 Search → Website UI
 Search → Icons
 Search → Dashboard
 Community resources can help you get inspiration and ready-made UI resources

Creatinng a Design File 
 A new design can be created using:
 Figma
   ↓
 New Design File

Figma can be used through:
 Web browser
 Desktop application

Figma Interface 
 The Figma workspace provides tools for creating and editing designs

Important things to understand:
 Canvas
 Frames
 Shapes
 Text
 Layers
 Design properties
 You can create elements using drag and drop and modify their properties

Frames 
 A Frame acts as a container/work area for designing a screen or part of a UI.

Examples:
 Desktop Frame
 Mobile Frame
 Tablet Frame

A frame can contain:
 Text
 Images
 Shapes
 Buttons
 Other design elements

For example:
 ┌─────────────────────────┐
 │        Header           │
 │                         │
 │   Welcome to Website    │
 │                         │
 │       [ Button ]        │
 │                         │
 └─────────────────────────┘
           Frame

Basic Design Tools 
 Figma provides tools for creating and modifying UI elements

Commonly used tools include:
 Move
 Scale
 Frame
 Shape
 Text
 These tools allow you to create layouts without writing code

Collaboration
 One of Figma's important features is collaboration
 Multiple people can work with the same design and give feedback

Comments
 Comments can be added directly to a design

Shortcut:
 C

Comments can be used for:
 Suggestions
 Feedback
 Design changes
 Discussions
 After a comment is addressed, it can be marked as resolved

Designing a website Prototype
  Before coding, you can create a visual representation of the website

For example:
 ┌─────────────────────────────┐
 │ Logo     Home  About  Login │
 ├─────────────────────────────┤
 │                             │
 │       Main Heading          │
 │                             │
 │        [ Button ]           │
 │                             │
 └─────────────────────────────┘
 This makes it easier to understand how the final website should look

Community UI Toolkits 
 Figma Community also contains UI kits and design resources

These can provide:
 Icons
 UI components
 Templates
 Design inspiration
 Ready-made layouts

 Some resources may be free while others may be paid

Exporting 
 Figma allows design elements/assets to be exported for use outside Figma
 The export feature is useful when you need assets for your actual website

 Figma Design
      ↓
 Select Asset
      ↓
 Export
      ↓
 Use Asset in Project

Multiple Pages
 A Figma file can contain multiple pages
 This helps organize different parts of a project

For example:
 Figma File
    │
    ├── Home Page
    ├── About Page
    ├── Login Page
    └── Contact Page

Using Figma for Real Projects 
 Figma is especially useful when someone gives you a design and asks you to convert it into a website.

Typical workflow:
 Design in Figma
       ↓
 Understand layout
       ↓
 Check colors / fonts / assets
       ↓
 Write HTML
       ↓
 Write CSS
       ↓
 Add JavaScript if required
       ↓
 Final Website

 A developer should therefore be comfortable reading and understanding a Figma design

Why Developers Should Learn Figma 
 Even if you are mainly a developer, knowing basic Figma is useful because:
 Clients may provide Figma designs
 Designers may work with developers using Figma
 You can understand UI layouts more easily
 You can inspect design assets before coding
 You can create your own website designs before implementation

